# All's Quiet on the Western Front: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], "western-front", "All's Quiet on the Western Front",
      "Seven players · thirteen years · every work built",
      "Seven players and no Warlord, so everything is fought over with grain. The throne changes hands nine times, "
      "most of them at the auction table, and all seven players sit on it at least once. Graham climbs from the "
      "wheat fields to the Scholar's stool and then to the throne in two years, and Paddy and Silke climb from a "
      "peasant's field to the chair too. At x6 the Harvest Pile runs dry and keeps paying on credit. Rich, the "
      "Gentry, falls to a rice field and climbs back to the throne, then builds the last work, the Marble Pagoda, "
      "one step before the auction where two players, each one Jar short of a set, could have taken it from him.",
      short="7 players")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Seven players: all seven provinces and no Warlord (rulebook Section 4, Note 2 and Appendix A). No raids and no soldiers, so this is the pure economic game. Every province holds four pieces of its own realia, and every public work in the book is owed.")
act("Seven booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Noah Tall the Scholar, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Graham Garner the Wheat Peasant.", map_hl=[1,2,3,4,5,6,7])
act("The Warlord's booklet goes back in the box, with the soldiers and the raid squares. Seven players is the one game with no Warlord (Note 2).")
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Each of the six realia provinces takes its four pieces of its own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three; Graham the Province 7 Reservoir, two by two. They're set aside until built.", map_hl=[2,3,7])
act("Noah, the Scholar, shuffles the twenty I Ching cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[4])
# From here the players' own choices.

# ===== CHAPTER 1: Whoever Waits Loses the Throne
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, honoured Official, learned Scholar, the harvest is in, and a fine one under so wise a throne. Silke, Paddy, Graham: I ask only the customary five this year, the least the law allows. Old land means a small rent. When the new fields open and you're farming more, we'll talk again, as friends.",
 "Rich: Five each is fifteen a year coming in, and it makes me look generous for nothing. The point is the promise I just made: rent goes up with the land, and I've said so in front of everyone, so nobody can call it a betrayal when I raise it.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks the minimum, and each peasant pays the Gentry 5.")
say("Reina", "A good harvest and a gracious Gentry. I'll set the tax at thirty percent this year, which is fair and leaves every granary full. The Summer Palace goes up first, because the Wall and the Canal can't start without it. Clark, Noah, you get ten each, double the law's minimum, because loyal service gets paid in this court. Remember that when the hard years come.",
 "Reina: Thirty percent brings in 108, and that leaves me about 138 after the palace and salaries. That's a cushion to start on the Wall and the canals, and nobody at the table has a reason to grumble. Paying my Official and Scholar double now costs ten grain, and it buys their loyalty for the year I have to raise the rate.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, eighteen a head, a hundred and eight for the crown, and not a grain short, my friends, because I count twice. Your Majesty, ten is a handsome salary, and I've written it down in the book where I keep the generous things. I keep another book too, for everything else.",
 "Clark: Year one I collect clean and I'm seen collecting clean. That way, the first time someone wants a little leniency at tax time, they'll know exactly what it's worth and who to pay for it.")
say("Reina", "Put it in the far southern corner, at J24. A palace should be quiet, and the good northern ground under the Wall stays free for the farms to come.",
 "Reina: A palace can't hold realia or a later work, so I'm putting it in a corner where it costs me nothing. The rows by the Wall stay open for the new land once the links are built.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="J24", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on J24, the far southern corner of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. New land, once it opens, is bought beyond a province's edge (the strip under the Wall, or the sea), never on its own squares. 1,010 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives a limit for each of the year's six lots at once, Province 2's Customs House to Province 7's Vase, and sets the minimum on its own. Each lot is then sold as a rising auction, in order. A tie goes to the earlier seat.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "The Customs House opens at three, friends. Every road to the throne runs past my desk, so think of it as a toll. As for the rest, I'm bidding sensibly. Anyone who wants a war over a jar can pay for it, and I'll make a note of who did.",
 "Clark: There are five pieces to collect and only forty-two grain to do it with, so I'm spreading small, cheap bids around and letting the hotheads pump grain into Rich's pile. Ten years is long, and an Official doesn't have to win early. He has to still be standing at the end.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, ten a head for your servants. That's a generous court, and I'll say so in every village in my province. Clark, a toll of three is very modest of you, and I'll pay it gladly if the table lets me. My Land Deeds go up at three, friends. A deed is a promise on paper, and I keep mine to the letter.",
 "Rich: I'm bidding a fair price on everything and won't get into a war over any of it. Grain thrown at the Harvest Pile now is grain I won't have for the new fields when the population doubles. Whoever wants a jar badly enough can have it, and I'll note who was so eager.")
say("Noah", "Here's the arithmetic, friends. Every lot that goes for ten instead of four puts six grain in the Harvest Pile and gives the seller only a grain and a half more. A war over one jar costs the whole table. I'm bidding small and plain, and my Brushes go up at three, the same as Clark and Rich set. Thank you, Your Majesty, for the ten. I'll read the I Ching honestly and keep the count true.",
 "Noah: I've got forty-two grain and need five pieces plus twenty in the bank. If I go above six on anything, my set costs more than I can save this year, so I'm buying cheaply wherever nobody else wants a fight, and walking away wherever someone does.")
act("Noah's sum, put right: a lot sold at 10 rather than 4 puts 4.5 more in the Harvest Pile and 1.5 more in the seller's hand.", map_hl=[4])
say("Silke", "Three, three and three, how neighbourly. My Dragon Jars open at four, friends, and before anyone grumbles, do the count: every set at this table needs one of my jars, and mine doesn't. Four is me being kind. I'm bidding plain on all of yours and won't chase anyone up the ladder, so let's all keep our grain out of Rich's Harvest Pile.",
 "Silke: Six sets, and five of them have to come through my jars, so I can ask a grain more than the men did and still look modest. I'm putting small, even bids on everything this year. I want a piece of every set before anyone starts paying war prices.")
act("The Harvest Pile is the common bank: Rich keeps it and pays the harvests out of it, but its grain isn't his.", map_hl=[3])
say("Paddy", "Three for my Bowls, same as the rest of you, and that's me not getting above myself. I'll bid honest and small on every lot, and if a neighbour wants a piece more than I do, they can have it with my blessing. There are three more years of auctions yet, and I'd rather get there with grain in the barn.",
 "Paddy: I need five pieces and twenty grain saved, and I've only got thirty-seven, so I'm keeping my limits low and taking whatever comes cheap. If I lose a fight over a jar this year I still have the grain I didn't spend, and I'll need it for land once the new fields open.")
say("Graham", "Three for my Vases, same as the rest of the table. I'll bid small, and if someone wants a piece more than I do, I'll wait. There are four years of auctions, and the pieces aren't going anywhere.",
 "Graham: Low ceilings on everything. If they all go cheap I've got most of a set and still have grain left, and if anyone pushes the price up I'll let them have it and remember who it was. Clark's 'other book' is worth watching too.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 3, Land Deed Scroll 3, Bamboo Brush 3, Dragon Jar 4, Wooden Bowl 3, Vase 3. Every limit at the table is between 4 and 6, so no lot comes near a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DC = "Take it, then. I've written down the price you paid."
DR = "Take it with my blessing. I'm a patient man, and there are three more of those in stock."
DN = "Too rich for me. Take it, and may it serve you well."
DS = "Take it, then. I've counted what you paid, and I'll remember it."
DP = "Go on, it's yours. I'll not throw good rice into Rich's pile chasing it."
DG = "Have it, then. I'll get one next year."
lot(2, "CH", [B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(3,5), B(3,None,DR), B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(6,5), B(6,None,DP), B(4,6)],
 hover="Rich, Silke and Paddy stood at 5; Noah's limit was 6. Noah puts the Customs House on K20.", at="K20")
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(4,5), B(4,None,DN), B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(2,6)],
 hover="Clark and Noah both had a limit of 6, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Clark, in Province 2, at 6. Clark puts the Land Deed Scroll on J12.", at="J12")
lot(4, "BB", [B(3,4), B(3,None,DR), B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(2,5)],
 hover="Clark and Rich both had a limit of 5; the tie goes to Clark, at 5. Clark puts the Bamboo Brush on J13.", at="J13")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,4), B(3,None,DR), B(4,4), B(4,None,DN), B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(2,5)],
 hover="Clark and Noah both had a limit of 5; the tie goes to Clark, at 5. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on J14.", at="J14")
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,4), B(3,None,DR), B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(2,4), B(2,None,DC), B(4,5)],
 hover="Everyone else stood at 4; Noah's limit was 5. Noah puts the Wooden Bowl on K21.", at="K21")
lot(7, "VA", [B(3,4), B(3,None,DR), B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(2,4), B(2,None,DC), B(4,5)],
 hover="Everyone else stood at 4; Noah's limit was 5. Noah puts the Vase on K22.", at="K22")
say("Clark", "A deed, a brush and a jar, all three filed neatly at the back of my province, well clear of the Wall. Noah, you've got my Customs House, so every time you look at it, remember who you bought it from. The tie went to my seat, I'm afraid. That's just procedure.",
 "Clark: Three of the five on the first night, at bargain prices, and the only man running level with me is the Scholar, who keeps the count. Now I need a Bowl and a Vase, and I'll keep a very close eye on Noah's purse.")
say("Noah", "First, a correction, since I'm the one who insists on the sums. I said a ten-grain lot puts six more in the Pile. It's four and a half, and the seller gets the other one and a half. I had it wrong and I'm glad it was put right. Sixteen spent for three pieces, which leaves me twenty-six, and Clark, you got my Brush fairly at five. Now I need a Land Deed and a Dragon Jar, and I'll pay a fair price for them next year.",
 "Noah: Three of my five pieces for sixteen grain, all of them in one tidy row away from the works. Clark beat me to the Deed and the Jar on the tie, so he's the one I'm watching now. He's buying the same set I am.")
act("Noah has 27, not 26: his quarter of the Brush's price, 1, came back to him.", map_hl=[4])
salaries({2:10, 4:10}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark and Noah 10 each, as she declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Not a cloud in months", "blight")
act("Noah turns the first I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest, and the population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "The I Ching for Year 2: 'Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year. All provinces collect an average harvest.' So it sounds grim, but it isn't: every province takes an average harvest. The population stays at one hundred million, the multiple stays at one, and everyone reads the x1 row of their income table. No province is named, so nobody plays a personal disaster. And thank you to the Storyteller: I had twenty-seven after the auctions, not twenty-six, because my quarter of the Brush came back to me.",
 "Noah: Another year at x1 means no new land yet, so the twenty grain I'm saving just sits in the barn waiting. I need a Deed and a Jar, and Clark has a head start on exactly the same set.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Drought on the card and still an average harvest. Heaven is kind to this court, Your Majesty. Friends, I said rent rises with the land, and there's no new land yet, so it's five again, the same as last year. Paid out in full from the Pile, thirty-five a head. Count it if you like.",
 "Rich: I kept my word last year and I'm keeping it now, because when the population doubles and those fields open, I want everyone to remember that Rich charged five for five years' worth of old land. I got nothing at the auctions, which hurts, but I have the most grain of anyone below the throne, and Clark and Noah are snapping up pieces I'll have to buy from them later.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at the minimum, and each peasant pays the Gentry 5.")
say("Reina", "The rate stays at thirty percent, the same as last year. On a drought year that's ten and a half a head, and nobody goes hungry for it. The crown is cutting both sections of the Grand Canal through Province 1 this year, eighty from my own purse, and Clark and Noah get ten each again. I said loyalty gets paid in this court, and I meant it.",
 "Reina: The canal adds twenty a year to the crown's harvest, which more than doubles the fifteen I get in an average year, so it pays for itself while the Wall waits. After this year's building and salaries the reserve should be about 106, and that's still enough to start the Wall.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Ten and a half from each of you, sixty-three for the crown, and I'll take the halves too, thank you, Graham. Your Majesty, a canal and two salaries in one year. That goes in the good book again. I'm starting to run out of pages in the other one.",
 "Clark: I'm two pieces short and the Scholar's hunting the very ones I've already got, so for now I stay the honest, dull tax collector. Honest records are what make a bent one believable later.")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: Reina builds both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace, which the Province 1 canal needs first, already stands. 930 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions, as last year: every seat but the Emperor's gives its limits for all six lots at once and sets the minimum on its own.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "Three for the Customs House again, same toll as always. Fair warning, friends: I want a Bowl and a Vase this year, and I'll pay properly for them. Paddy, Graham, if you'd rather sell to the Official than make an enemy of him, you know where I sit.",
 "Clark: Two pieces and twenty grain stand between me and a set, so I'm bidding hard on the Bowl and the Vase. I'm also quietly ready to outbid Noah for the Deed and the Jar, because a Scholar with a full set is a Scholar who wants my job.")
say("Rich", "A canal through the north-east, Your Majesty. The whole Wall country will be singing about it. Clark, my dear Official, you warned us honestly, so I'll do the same: I came away with nothing last year, and I don't mean to do it twice. My Deeds open at three, as before.",
 "Rich: Clark already has a Deed, a Brush and a Jar, so he's halfway to a set, and he's told the whole table he wants a Bowl and a Vase. I'll pay a bit over the odds to keep those two away from him, because I can afford a few grain more than I can afford Clark sitting on the throne.")
say("Noah", "Plain numbers, as always. I need two pieces, a Deed and a Jar, and I'm bidding on nothing else, so the Customs House, the Bowls and the Vases are all yours without me in the way. Clark already has his Deed and Jar, so it's only the peasants and Rich I'd be meeting there. If you'll let me have the one Deed and the one Jar quietly, I'll stay out of every other lot this year, and none of us feeds the Pile. My Brushes open at three again.",
 "Noah: Two pieces from a full set, with sixty-one in the barn and twenty of that set aside, I can afford to pay properly this once. The race that matters is with Clark, who still needs a Bowl and a Vase, and I'm not touching those.")
say("Silke", "Last year I bid nice and small and went home with nothing, while Clark and Noah filled their shelves. This year I'm bidding what the pieces are worth. My Jars open at five. Noah, you'll want one, and so will Rich, Paddy and Graham. That's four of you after three jars, so five is a bargain.",
 "Silke: Every set needs one of my Jars, so I can price mine up while I bid properly on everyone else's. Rich is sitting on ninety-eight grain and needs the same pieces I do, so he's the one I'm watching.")
say("Paddy", "I came home with an empty cart last year too, Silke, so I'll bid a bit firmer this time, though not daft. Clark, you'll get no enemy in me over a Bowl. They open at four, and if you bid fair for one it's yours, gladly.",
 "Paddy: There are only three of each piece left and five of us still chasing them, so bidding small won't do again. I've put down a fair price on every lot and kept back enough grain for a field when the new land opens.")
say("Graham", "I came away with nothing last year as well, and I sold my Vase for five to a man who isn't even buying Vases. So I'll bid a little firmer this year. Clark, the Vases open at four. If you want one, bid fair and it's yours.",
 "Graham: Last year proved that bidding small gets you nothing, so I've raised every ceiling a few grain, and the Jar most of all, since four of us are after three of them. If it all goes my way I still keep more than twenty grain back for the land when it opens.")
act("Noah's set does need a Vase: his own type is the Bamboo Brush, so the Vase he bought last year is one of his five. The minimums: Customs House 3, Land Deed 3, Brush 3, Dragon Jar 5, Wooden Bowl 4, Vase 4. No lot is close enough at the top for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DC = "Fine, take it. I've noted the price in my little book."
DR = "Well bid. I'll get my turn, I always do."
DN = "That's more than it's worth to me. It's yours, and well bid."
DS = "Have it, then. I can count, and I'll see what you paid for it."
DP = "That's past what it's worth to me. Good luck to you with it."
DG = "Go on, then. I'll remember the price."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,7), B(5,None,DS), B(6,7), B(6,None,DP), B(7,7), B(7,None,DG), B(3,8)],
 hover="Rich and Silke both had a limit of 8, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Rich, at 8. Rich puts the Customs House on M13.", at="M13")
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,7,None,"Clark's 7 was a keep-from: he bid only to stop Noah."), B(2,None,DC), B(5,7), B(5,None,DS), B(6,7), B(6,None,DP), B(7,7), B(7,None,DG), B(4,8)],
 hover="Noah's limit was 12; nobody else went past 7. Noah puts the Land Deed Scroll on K23.", at="K23")
lot(4, "BB", [B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(7,6), B(7,None,DG), B(5,7), B(5,None,DS), B(3,8)],
 hover="Rich and Silke both had a limit of 8; the tie goes to Rich, at 8. Rich puts the Bamboo Brush on M14.", at="M14")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,7,None,"Clark's 7 was a keep-from, to stop Noah."), B(2,None,DC), B(3,8), B(3,None,DR), B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(7,8), B(7,None,DG), B(4,9)],
 hover="Rich, Paddy and Graham stood at 8; Noah's limit was 14. Noah puts the Dragon Jar on K24.", at="K24")
say("Rich", "Clark, your Customs House is in good hands, and Noah, your Brush too, both safe at the back of my province, well away from the Wall. And Clark, congratulations on the Bowl and the Vase. Twelve apiece, a princely sum, and the Harvest Pile thanks you for every grain.",
 "Rich: I only got two pieces, but Clark paid twenty-four for his two, and I'm the one who made him pay it. He's now holding four of his five and has about thirty-eight grain left, so he's the man to watch, and I'm the man with the money to stop him.")
say("Noah", "Then I'll say it plainly, because I've never hidden a sum at this table. That's a Customs House, a Bowl, a Vase, a Deed and a Jar: all five pieces. I spent seventeen, my quarter of the Brush brings back two, and that leaves forty-six and a half, well over the twenty. So the Scholar holds a full set. Silke, Paddy, Graham, I'm sorry you've gone home empty twice now. I won't pretend that's fair luck.",
 "Noah: Five pieces in one row from K20 to K24, and twenty grain to spare, so from now on I can replace any seat at this table, the throne included. Everyone will be counting me now, so I'll keep telling them the truth and let them decide whether to fear it.")
act("Noah's set is complete after the fifth lot, with the Bowl and the Vase still to be sold. A player with a complete set and 20 grain may change places at any moment (Section 11), so the Storyteller asks him whether he does it now.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "I won't dress it up. Clark holds a Deed, a Brush, a Jar, a Bowl and a Vase, which is a full set, and he has the grain to use it, so whoever waits loses the throne. Your Majesty, you paid me well and kept every word you gave, and I'm sorry it's you. I take the crown, my five pieces go home to their provinces, and you draw sixty and take the Scholar's chair. Clark, I'll pay you your ten this year as promised, and every year after. That's a promise, and I keep mine.",
 "Noah: Clark could have swapped seats with the throne the moment his Vase landed, so I've moved first, and I've done it out loud. Now I've got the crown reserve, nine hundred and thirty of works still owed, and a table that knows exactly how I got here.")
swap(4, 1, "Noah Tall shows the Scholar's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 46.5 grain, after the fifth lot and before the Bowl and the Vase are sold, and takes the throne. He returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 126. Reina Osorio draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Scholar, in Province 4, from now on.",
     hover="Clark has won the Bowl and the Vase on the limits already given, but they aren't sold or placed yet, so his set isn't complete when Noah moves.")
act("Reina is a bidder now, so before the last two lots are sold she gives her limits on them too.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "Well. Noah, that was neatly done, and I'll remember you said sorry before you took it. I'm in the Scholar's chair now and I'm starting my collection over. And Clark, you're two pieces short of a full set, and I can count too. Nobody here should hand you the Bowl and the Vase cheap.",
 "Reina: Clark already holds a Deed, a Brush and a Jar, so this Bowl and Vase would give him a set and he could take anybody's seat, mine included. I'll pay up to twenty on either one to keep it away from him. Anyone else can have them cheap.")
DE = "Take it, then. I'll be watching where it goes."
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,7), B(7,None,DG), B(3,8), B(3,None,DR), B(5,8), B(5,None,DS), B(2,14), B(2,None,DC), B(4,15)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich's keep-from stopped at 11 and Clark's limit was 14; Reina would have gone to 20 to keep it from Clark."),
          ("Reina", "That was thirty grain spent to stop Clark finishing his set this year, and they're two of the five pieces I need for my own way back to the throne.")), at="N21")
lot(7, "VA", [B(6,7), B(6,None,DP), B(3,8), B(3,None,DR), B(5,8), B(5,None,DS), B(2,14), B(2,None,DC), B(4,15)],
 hover="The same climb: Clark to 14, Reina to 15. Reina puts the Vase on N22, beside the Bowl, in the far corner of Province 4.", at="N22")
say("Reina", "Fifteen apiece. That's dear, I know, but Clark, I warned you I can count. The Bowl and the Vase can sit quietly at the back of the Scholar's province while I collect the other three.",
 "Reina: I put them in the far corner so they won't block any canal Noah might want through Province 4.")
say("Noah", "Ten each, exactly what was declared at the start of this year. Clark, I promised you ten and here it is. Reina, the Scholar's chair was promised ten, and I won't take that back just because you're the one sitting in it. That leaves the crown a hundred and fifty-two and a half against nine hundred and thirty of works still owed, and I'll tell you every year how far along we are.",
 "Noah: Paying Reina ten helps the one player who's openly rebuilding a set to come for me, but the court's word was ten and I'm keeping it. My real worry is Clark: he's two pieces short, he's sixty-four grain richer, and he's annoyed.")
salaries({2:10, 4:10}, text="Salaries: Noah, now the Emperor, pays Clark and the new Scholar, Reina, 10 each, as Reina declared at the start of the year.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: Four Emperors and a Vase
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "The public utility tax", "utility")
act("Reina, the Scholar, turns the card: the Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest, and the population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "The I Ching, Year Three: Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest, then pays the Emperor a 2 million utility tax, and any province with Public Works pays 2 million more. The counter reads 100 million, so the multiple is still x1. Read the x1 row, everyone. Noah, that's a nice little windfall for you, and I'm sure you'll remember the hand that turned the card when salaries come round.",
 "Reina: No disaster this year, so nobody gets an excuse to cry poor, and the levy lands right in Noah's lap. With him that much richer, five grain for his Scholar would look stingy in front of the whole table, and I want everyone watching what he pays me.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("The Utility Tax: each of Provinces 2 to 7 pays the Emperor 2. Only Province 1 has public works so far, and it is the Emperor's own, so its levy is paid to itself.", "Noah takes 12. No other province has a work yet, so nobody else pays the extra 2.", {4:12,1:-2,2:-2,3:-2,5:-2,6:-2,7:-2}, map_hl=[1,2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Rich", "Thirty-five to every hand at this table, counted out to the last grain. Silke, Paddy, Graham, it's five again this year, the same as before, because the land is the same as before. I gave my word on that and I'll keep it. And Your Majesty, a dozen from the Utility Tax suits the throne very well. I only hope some of it finds its way to the works you owe.",
 "Rich: Rent stays at five because no new fields have opened, and I promised it would only rise with the land. Once the counter doubles I'll raise it, and nobody will be able to say I broke my word. Meanwhile Noah keeps taking in grain and hasn't built a thing, and I'm counting what he owes.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at the minimum, and each peasant pays the Gentry 5.")
say("Noah", "Here are the sums, all of them. Thirty percent of thirty-five is ten and a half from each of you, sixty-three in all. The crown had a hundred and ninety-nine and a half, so with the tax that's two sixty-two and a half. Sixty goes on the Reservoir in Graham's province, which is twenty more harvest for him every year, and twenty goes to Clark and Reina, ten each as I promised. That leaves a hundred and eighty-two and a half, and the works owed come down from nine hundred and thirty to eight-seventy. Reina, I haven't forgotten whose hand turned the card. Graham, this one's yours, and Silke and Paddy, the Winter Palace comes next so your canals can follow.",
 "Noah: Rich has been telling the peasants the crown keeps its grain. A reservoir they can see answers that better than anything I could say, and Graham gets an extra twenty every harvest from now on. I kept the tax at thirty so nobody can say I took more than the land can give, and I'm keeping the rest in reserve because when the population doubles I'll need it for new land.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Ten and a half from each of you, the half included, and yes, that means you too, Reina. It goes into my hands first and then on to the crown, so let's keep the counting clean. Your Majesty, sixty-three, delivered to the grain. I trust the ten will come back to me just as promptly at year's end. I'll have it written down either way.",
 "Clark: Every grain of the crown's money passes through these hands. That's worth more than ten a year, and Noah ought to start feeling it. This year I keep quiet about the Bowl and the Vase, and I sit on a hundred and seven grain until the auction.")
say("Noah", "I'm putting it in the far corner, R17 to S18. That's four squares out of thirty-four, and it leaves Graham the whole middle of the province for his Vases. Graham, if you'd rather have it somewhere else, tell me now, and I'll move it before the year closes.",
 "Noah: Every square the reservoir sits on is a square Graham can't use, so I've tucked it into the corner where it gets in his way the least. It's meant to be a gift, and a gift shouldn't cramp the man it's for.")
build("Province 7 Reservoir", 60, {1:60}, key="res7", at="R17:S18", text="Public works: Noah builds the Province 7 Reservoir at the crown's cost of 60, on R17:S18. It adds 20 to Province 7's harvest from next year.", hover="Noah pays the 60, into the Harvest Pile. Province 7 has 28 squares, not 34; the Reservoir takes 4 of them, leaving 24. 870 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions, as last year: every seat but the Emperor's gives its limits for all six lots at once and sets the minimum on its own.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "The Customs House opens at three, as always. A toll's a toll. As for the rest, I have nothing to announce this year, gentlemen and ladies. I learned that lesson at a price, and I've written it down.",
 "Clark: Last year I told the whole table what I wanted and Reina took it from me by a single grain, so tonight I say nothing and put thirty-six on the Bowl and thirty-six on the Vase. Win both and I still have twenty-four and a half left, enough for the set and the twenty it costs, and I'll be one turn from deciding who sits where.")
say("Rich", "Clark, my dear friend, you know I'd love to see you finish your set, truly I would. But a Bowl and a Vase are exactly what a humble landlord needs as well, and I'd be a poor businessman if I didn't put up a fair price for them. My Scrolls go up at four, which is a bargain for good title to land.",
 "Rich: Clark is two pieces from replacing anyone at this table, so I'll pay more to stop him than I'd ever pay for the pieces myself. Even if I lose every one of those auctions, I keep the twenty grain the set needs.")
say("Reina", "Five lots I can bid on, and only three I want. You all know which three, so there's no point pretending otherwise. I'll pay a fair price for each and not a grain more. Whoever wants a bidding war with the Scholar is only feeding the Harvest Pile. My Brushes go up at 2, and I hope they find good homes.",
 "Reina: Sixty-two and a half grain, three pieces still to buy, and I have to keep twenty back. That puts my ceiling at fourteen a lot. Rich and Clark can afford to outbid me, so I'm counting who goes after the Jar, and I'm watching Clark's Bowl and Vase, because I can't afford to block him this year.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Silke: twelve lots have been sold and no peasant has won one. A peasant's set can replace the Official, the Gentry or the Scholar (not the Emperor directly), and at the end of Year 2 the Scholar's seat was held by the player with the least grain at the table.", map_hl=[5])
say("Silke", "Two years now I've watched the top table buy everything at eight and win the ties by where they happen to sit. Not this year. I'm in on every piece I need, and Jars start at six. Rich, Reina, you'll both want one before long, so it's cheaper to buy today than at the end of the game.",
 "Silke: Odd numbers, and every one above the eight that Rich keeps winning with. Even if I win all five I'm left with twenty-eight, and that covers the twenty grain the set needs. I'm watching Reina most. She's at sixty-two and a half and needs the same Customs House and Deed I do, and her seat is the one I can take.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Rich, five it is and here it is, counted. And Your Majesty, I'll hold you to that canal. This year I'm not chasing every piece on the table like a dog after a cart. I'm after two, and I'll say plainly which: a Customs House and a Brush. Reina, Clark, I'd sooner pay you fair than have the rest of them push it up for nothing.",
 "Paddy: Two lots I mean to win, the Brush most of all, because there's only two left and only three of us want one. If I take both at my limits I've still got better than thirty put by for a field, and I won't touch that.")
say("Graham", "The corner's fine where it is, Your Majesty. Thank you. Twenty a year is twenty a year. And Rich, five again, paid. I'm bidding this year, so nobody should be surprised when I don't stop at seven.",
 "Graham: This year it's the Deed and the Jar. I'll go to eighteen on each, which is what this table charges, and put a sensible bid on the rest in case the others are out bidding each other on something else. Whatever happens I keep twenty back for the set, and with the Reservoir coming in, next year I'll have more to spend than Paddy or Silke.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 3, Land Deed 4, Brush 2, Dragon Jar 6, Wooden Bowl 4, Vase 5.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DC = "Take it, then. I've made a note of the price, and of who paid it."
DR = "Take it, then. I hope it serves you better than it would have served me."
DE = "Take it, then. You paid three quarters of that to the Harvest Pile, and I'll still be here next year."
DS = "Fine. Keep it, there's a second and a third in stock, and every grain you spend now is grain you won't have later."
DP = "Too rich for a rice farmer. Good luck to you with it."
DG = "Go on, then. I'll buy the next one."
lot(2, "CH", [B(7,8), B(7,None,DG), B(5,11), B(5,None,DS), B(4,14), B(4,None,DE), B(6,15)],
 hover="Reina stopped at 14; Paddy's limit was 20. Paddy puts the Customs House on S25.", at="S25")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,11), B(5,None,DS), B(4,14), B(4,None,DE), B(7,15)],
 hover="Reina stopped at 14; Graham's limit was 18. Graham puts the Land Deed Scroll on P15.", at="P15")
lot(4, "BB", [B(5,9), B(5,None,DS), B(7,9), B(7,None,DG), B(6,10)],
 hover="Silke and Graham stopped at 9; Paddy's limit was 24. Paddy puts the Bamboo Brush on S24.", at="S24")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(4,14), B(4,None,DE), B(7,18), B(7,None,DG), B(3,19)],
 hover="Reina stopped at 14 and Graham at 18; Rich's limit was 25. Rich puts the Dragon Jar on M12.", at="M12")
act("The Wooden Bowl and the Vase are close at the top: Clark's limit of 36 against Rich's 35, a keep-from to stop Clark. So the two of them get one live raise each. Rich stands on the Bowl and raises the Vase to 60; Clark raises both to 40.", map_hl=[2,3])
say("Rich", "Clark, old friend, have the Bowl with my blessing. A fine piece for a fine Official. But I'm afraid I've grown very attached to that Vase, and I'll pay handsomely for my sentimentality.",
 "Rich: Clark only needs to win both, and I only need to win one. If he chases me past sixty on the Vase, the Bowl and the Vase together leave him without the twenty grain the set needs, so either way he stays one step short this year.")
say("Clark", "Rich, my dear friend, you'd love to see me finish my set, so here's your chance to watch it happen. A humble landlord still needs a Deed, a Bowl and a Vase. An Official needs two pieces and nothing else, and he's the one who'll be counting your tax next year.",
 "Clark: Forty on each takes me to exactly the twenty grain the set costs, and a tie goes to my seat, so Rich has to beat forty on both to stop me. He's three pieces short and I'm two, so I'll pay whatever it takes up to that line.")
act("Rich doesn't need a Deed: he holds the Customs House and the Brush and has just won the Jar, so he needs only a Bowl and a Vase.", map_hl=[3])
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,8), B(7,None,DG), B(5,9), B(5,None,DS), B(3,35), B(3,None,DR), B(2,36)],
 hover="Rich stood at 35; Clark raised to 40, so Clark takes it at 36 and puts the Wooden Bowl on J15.", at="J15")
lot(7, "VA", [B(5,9), B(5,None,DS), B(2,40), B(2,None,DC), B(3,41)],
 hover="Clark raised to 40, Rich to 60, so Rich takes it at 41 and puts the Vase on L13.", at="L13")
say("Clark", "J15, right next to the Jar, where I can keep an eye on it. Rich, 'sentimental' is a lovely word for forty-one grain, and I've written it down exactly as you said it. So we each need one piece now, and we each know who's selling it. Graham, Paddy, I'd make sure your prices are posted early next year.",
 "Clark: Now it's a straight race: I need a Vase from Graham and Rich needs a Bowl from Paddy, and whoever gets his piece first decides who sits where. Rich goes in the other book, right under Reina, for forty-one grain of sentimentality.")
say("Rich", "Clark, my dear fellow, you'll count my tax very handsomely, I'm sure, and I'll pay every grain of it. So now we each want a single Bowl, and Paddy has two left. What a pleasant coincidence for Paddy. Silke, Graham, thank you both, those are lovely pieces.",
 "Rich: Clark and I are now even, four pieces each, and both of us waiting on one of Paddy's Bowls. So the man who matters next year is the Rice Peasant, and I'm his landlord.")
act("Clark needs a Vase, not a Bowl: his Bowl is on J15. Rich needs a Bowl (Paddy has 2 in stock); Clark needs a Vase (Graham has 2 in stock).", map_hl=[2,3,6,7])
say("Paddy", "Well now. Two years with an empty cart and now I've two pieces at once. Clark, Reina, thank you kindly, and fair prices both. I'll set them down in the corner by the water, out of the way of any canal His Majesty might be sending my way.",
 "Paddy: Twenty-five grain for two pieces, when I'd braced for forty-four, so the field money's safe and then some. I've put them tight in the far corner so the open middle stays free for land and works later.")
say("Graham", "Fifteen. That's the price at this table, so that's what I paid. Rich, it's going in the middle of my land, where a deed belongs.",
 "Graham: One piece in hand, and it's the right one. It goes in the middle, so the Jar can go next to it if the Jar comes my way, and whatever happens I've kept my twenty back.")
salaries({2:10, 4:10}, text="Salaries: Noah pays Clark and Reina 10 each, as he declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "Fat years make large families", "double2")
act("Reina, the Scholar, turns the card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles to x2, 200 million. Every province's consumption doubles with it, and new land opens (Section 13).", map_hl=[4], big=True)
say("Reina", "The I Ching, Year Four: Population Doubles. Fat years make large families. Every province gets a good harvest this year, and then every mouth counts twice. The counter goes to 200 million, so it's x2 from now on. Everyone move to the x2 row of your income table. New land is open at 20 a square, and the throne may pay part of it. Your Majesty, a province that can't feed itself won't pay you tax, so I'd think hard about helping with that bill.",
 "Reina: At x2 I'm five grain a year in the red until I buy land, so a square comes first and the shelves can wait. Clark needs only a Vase and Rich needs only a Bowl, so whichever of them finishes first takes somebody's seat. I want Noah worried about that as much as I am.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "A good year, friends, and I've paid it out to the grain. Silke, Paddy, it stays at five, because your fields are the same fields they were. Graham, His Majesty has given you a Reservoir and you've reaped forty to everyone else's twenty, and I said from the first day that the rent would rise with the land. Ten, as friends, and it'll be ten for every one of you the day your new fields come in.",
 "Rich: Graham's land is the only land that's grown, so his is the only rent that rises, exactly as I promised. And I'm not going to squeeze the one man at this table who sells Wooden Bowls.")
act("Rent above the minimum of 5 is a negotiation, so Graham may pay the 10 Rich asks or only the 5 the rules require.", map_hl=[3,7])
say("Graham", "Five, Rich, counted. You said last year the rent stays the same because the land's the same, and it is the same land. The Reservoir was His Majesty's grain, not yours. When I've bought new fields, you can ask me again. Your Majesty, I'm buying two squares on the southern edge, and I'm asking the crown to pay half. That's twenty a year more for me to pay tax on.",
 "Graham: Rich wants paying for a reservoir he didn't build, and I've written that down. I'm buying two squares now while they're cheap, because when that counter gets to x4 I'll need them.")
rents(5, "Rents: each peasant pays the Gentry 5. Graham declines the extra 5 Rich asked of him.")
act("Two squares of new land add 40 a year to Province 7's harvest (20 each), not 20.", map_hl=[7])
say("Noah", "Here are the sums. Thirty percent comes to forty-two, so the crown stands at two twenty-four and a half. The Winter Palace is twenty, and it opens the canals, so Paddy and Silke, that's the canal I promised, and it's on its way. Salaries are twenty, the same ten each as always, and my own two squares are forty. That leaves a hundred and forty-four and a half. On new land, the crown pays half of every square, ten a square, up to two squares each, for everyone at this table, not just Graham. If all six of you take your two, that's a hundred and twenty and I'm left with twenty-four and a half, but I won't promise grain I can't pay. Works owed come down to eight-fifty.",
 "Noah: The doubling hit me hardest: my own province now runs at minus twenty-five a year, so I'm buying land I need for myself while I pay half of everyone else's. Capping it at two squares each means I can pay every grain I've promised even if all six of them take me up on it, and I'd rather be thin than be a liar.")
act("Only Silke, Paddy, Graham and Reina can buy new land this year: Clark's and Rich's strips open only once their four Wall links stand (0 of 4 each). So the crown's half on two squares each comes to at most 80, not 120.", map_hl=[2,3])
say("Noah", "Thank you, Storyteller, and I'll correct myself in front of everyone. Only four of you can buy land this year, so the most the crown can owe on the half-shares is eighty, not a hundred and twenty. Even if all four of you take two squares, the crown ends the year at sixty-four and a half. The Winter Palace goes on J23, next to the Summer Palace, and my two squares are J25 and K25.",
 "Noah: I keep the two palaces side by side so the works stay in one place, and I put my new fields on the southern edge of Province 1, closest to the provinces I'm trying to help. I got my own sum wrong out loud and corrected it out loud, because that's the promise I made.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Six from each of you, twelve from Graham since he took the double harvest, forty-two for the crown, counted and delivered. Your Majesty, your half-share on new land is very generous, but Rich and I can't buy a single square until the Wall above us goes up. So I'll say it once, politely and in front of everyone: the man who collects your tax is losing grain every year he collects it. I'd like to see the first Wall link in Province 2 on next year's list.",
 "Clark: Without new land I'm five a year in the red. The only fix is Wall above me, and the throne won't spend a grain on that unless someone keeps saying so in public. A man who carries the crown's money in both hands gets to make some noise about it.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="J23", text="Public works: Noah builds the Winter Palace on J23, beside the Summer Palace, at the crown's cost of 20. The canals in Provinces 4, 5 and 6 may now be dug.", hover="Noah pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 850 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["J25", "K25"], {1:40}, "New land: Noah buys two squares of sea for Province 1, at J25 and K25, for 40 from the crown. Each adds 20 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.")
buy_land(7, ["T15", "T16"], {7:20, 1:20}, "New land: Graham buys two squares for Province 7, at T15 and T16, paying 20; the crown pays the other 20, half of each. Each adds 20 to Province 7's harvest from next year.")
act("New land, for each province that can buy: Silke, Paddy and Reina are asked, as Graham was.", map_hl=[4,5,6])
say("Reina", "Your Majesty, that's a generous offer, ten a square, and I'd take it gladly, except I have no square to buy. J25 and K25 were the only places my province touched the sea, and you've just bought them both for Province 1. So the Scholar is boxed in, eating at x2 with no new field anywhere. I'm not accusing anyone. I'm saying it out loud so it goes in Clark's book next to everything else.",
 "Reina: He took my only coastline, whether he meant to or not, so I'm five a year in the red with nowhere to grow. The only way out now is the set, and that means a Customs House, a Deed and a Jar before Clark or Rich finishes theirs.")
act("Reina buys no land: Province 4 touched the sea only at J25 and K25, which are now Province 1's, so no free square touches it.", map_hl=[1,4])
say("Paddy", "Your Majesty, I'll take you up on the half, on two squares, ten apiece, and I'll pay the third myself, the whole twenty. That's three fields along the shore, T25, T26 and S26, and every one of them will have a tax on it next year, so the crown gets its ten back soon enough. Rich, when they've come in, ask me about the rent and you'll get a straight answer.",
 "Paddy: The card says x4 would sink me unless I've five more fields, so I'm buying three now while the crown pays half, and I'm still keeping thirty put by. A man who owns his fields can sleep at night.")
buy_land(6, ["T25", "T26", "S26"], {6:40, 1:20}, "New land: Paddy buys three squares for Province 6, at T25, T26 and S26, paying 40; the crown pays 20, half of two of them. Each adds 20 to Province 6's harvest from next year.")
say("Silke", "Your Majesty, I'll take your half and then some. Three squares on my coast: L26, M26 and N26. I pay thirty and the crown pays thirty, which is ten a square on the two you offered and ten on the third. Count it however you like, but every extra square on my coast is twenty more a year for Clark to tax, and the Winter Palace canal will need fields at the other end of it.",
 "Silke: My card has me losing five a year as things stand, and if the population doubles again I'm short by five squares, so I'm buying ahead while the crown is still paying half. That leaves me about sixty for the auctions. I'll bid less on the pieces this year, but the fields will still be paying when the bidding wars are over.")
say("Noah", "Silke, it's twenty, ten a square on two, as I said. Paddy paid his own third square in full on the same offer, and I won't give one of you a better deal than the other. Reina, you're right, and I didn't see it until you said it: I bought the only two squares your province touched. That's my mistake, and I'll find a way to make it right with you before next year's land. Clark, I've heard you on the Wall, and it goes on next year's list. With salaries paid, the crown ends the year at eighty-four and a half.",
 "Noah: Paying Silke's third square would have punished Paddy for being honest about what he asked for, so the answer is no. Boxing Reina in was a real blunder on my part, and I'd rather own it in front of everyone than let her own it for me.")
say("Silke", "Fair enough, Your Majesty. Same terms as Paddy, and I'll pay the third square in full myself. L26, M26 and N26, sixty grain of coastline for forty out of my own pocket. Rich, ask me about the rent once they've come in, not before.",
 "Silke: It leaves me fifty and a half, which is thin for the auctions. But a field that pays sixty a year from now on is worth more to me than one Vase bought too dear, and if the population doubles again the peasants without land will be selling to me.")
buy_land(5, ["L26", "M26", "N26"], {5:40, 1:20}, "New land: Silke buys three squares of sea for Province 5, at L26, M26 and N26, paying 40; the crown pays 20, half of two of them. Each adds 20 to Province 5's harvest from next year.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives its limits for all six lots at once and sets the minimum on its own.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "Customs House at three, as always. Reina, I've written it in the book, the throne buying up your coastline. That entry isn't under your name, for once. Graham, I'll say it plainly this year, since there's no secret left to keep: I want your Vase, and I'll pay a good deal more than fifteen for it.",
 "Clark: Sixty on the Vase still leaves me twenty-eight, enough for the set and its twenty. Silke and Paddy can't come near that with what they hold. I'm letting Rich go after his Bowl, because chasing it would cost me the Vase. This year it's a race, and I mean to finish first.")
say("Rich", "Graham, five it is, and you were quite right to hold me to the letter. When your two southern fields come in I'll be asking, and you've told me yourself you'll give me an answer. Paddy, one Bowl is all I want from this whole table this year, and I'll pay a good honest price for it, so I hope you'll let it go to a friend. My Deeds go up at two, which is cheap for good title.",
 "Rich: One Bowl finishes my set, and I've made sure that however the bidding goes I still have the twenty grain to stand up with it. I'd love to keep Clark from his Vase too, but not at the cost of my own crown.")
say("Reina", "My last Brush goes up at 2. Paddy already has one, so whoever's still short, it's yours cheap. I'm bidding on the Customs House, the Deed and the Jar this year and nothing else. Silke, Graham, Paddy, every grain we push each other up goes three quarters into the Pile. If you let me have one of these cleanly, I'll stand aside for you on the next one, and I'll say so in front of everybody. Your Majesty, I'm holding you to 'make it right.'",
 "Reina: No land to buy means my grain does nothing in the granary, so all of it goes on the shelves, while still keeping the twenty the set needs. Clark and Rich are each one piece away, and I can't afford to block them and still finish my own set, so I'm racing them instead.")
say("Silke", "Rich, Clark, I'm not getting between you and the last piece each of you needs. The Bowl and the Vase are yours to fight over, and I'll watch the Harvest Pile get fatter with everyone else. The last Brush is a different story. Graham, there's only one left, and I'd rather settle it with you over the table than let it go to the Pile.",
 "Silke: I spent my bidding grain on land, so this year I'm going after the one piece nobody else can supply: the last Bamboo Brush, with Graham as my only rival. I'm passing on the Bowl and the Vase because Rich and Clark will bid them past anything I could pay, and every grain they spend there is a grain they won't have later.")
say("Paddy", "My purse went into the ground this year, so I'll be bidding small and plain, a Deed if Rich will part with one fair, and a Jar if it comes cheap. Rich, the Bowl's at fifteen, the same price everyone at this table seems to agree on. Clark, I'll stay well out of your way on the Vase.",
 "Paddy: I spent the fat on fields, so it's thin bids this year and a fair price on the Bowl, since Rich needs it and nobody else much does. I'd sooner wait a year for the Jar than go into debt for it.")
say("Graham", "One Brush left in the game, and two of us need it. Silke, I'll say it plainly: I'm going high for it, so if you want to save your grain, take the Bowl and the Customs House instead. Clark, the Vase opens at twenty. You paid thirty-six for a Bowl, so I don't think twenty will trouble you.",
 "Graham: The Brush is the one that matters, because there's only one left and whoever misses it can't finish the set. Clark needs only my Vase, so he'll pay for it, and a quarter of what he pays comes to me.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 3, Land Deed 2, Brush 2, Dragon Jar 5, Wooden Bowl 15, Vase 20.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DC = "Noted, with the price and the name beside it."
DR = "Well bid. Enjoy it, and I'll be back for the next one."
DE = "Yours, and fairly won. I'll be back for the next one."
DS = "Take it, then. I've got sixty a year coming in off the coast, and I'll still be here next year."
DP = "That's past what a rice farmer can spend this year. It's yours, and good luck to you."
DG = "It's yours. I'll wait for the next one."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,9), B(5,None,DS), B(7,16), B(7,None,DG), B(4,17)],
 hover="Graham stopped at 16; Reina's limit was 22. Reina puts the Customs House on L20.", at="L20")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,11), B(5,None,DS), B(6,14), B(6,None,DP), B(4,15)],
 hover="Paddy stopped at 14; Reina's limit was 20. Reina puts the Land Deed Scroll on L21.", at="L21")
lot(4, "BB", [B(5,23,None,"Silke's last 2 were a keep-from, to stop Graham."), B(5,None,DS), B(7,24)],
 hover="Silke went to 23 to keep it from Graham; Graham's limit was 32. Graham puts the last Bamboo Brush on P16.", at="P16")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,10), B(6,None,DP), B(7,17), B(7,None,DG), B(4,18)],
 hover="Graham stopped at 17; Reina's limit was 22. Reina puts the Dragon Jar on L22.", at="L22")
say("Graham", "Twenty-four. Thank you, Reina, that was the last one in the game, and it was a fair price. Silke, I'm sorry. I did tell you plainly beforehand.",
 "Graham: That's the Deed and the Brush next to each other in the middle, and nobody else at this table can buy a Brush now. Three pieces to go, and they'll cost what they cost, one at a time.")
act("Reina's set is complete after the fifth lot, with the Bowl and the Vase still to be sold, so the Storyteller asks her whether she changes places now.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "Customs House, Deed, Jar, next to my Bowl and Vase. That's five, with grain to spare. Noah, two years ago you took this chair in the middle of an auction and said sorry while you did it. I'm taking it back the same way, and I'm sorry too. The Scholar's seat is a good one: the Emperor pays you at least five, and I'll pay you ten, because I've always paid loyal service double. The province is a little short of coastline, but you know that better than anyone. Clark, Rich, the Bowl and the Vase are all yours. Bid carefully, because the throne isn't empty any more.",
 "Reina: I took the throne before lot 6 is sold, so whichever of Clark and Rich finishes next has to come after me, and I go in holding both reserves. Noah gets the landlocked province he made, and I'll pay him ten so the table remembers which of us keeps her word.")
swap(4, 1, "Reina Osorio shows the Scholar's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 45 grain, after the fifth lot and before the Bowl and the Vase are sold, and takes the throne back. She returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 104.5. Noah Tall draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Scholar, in Province 4, from now on.",
     hover="Rich and Clark have won the Bowl and the Vase on the limits already given, but they aren't sold yet, so neither set is complete when Reina moves.")
act("Noah is a bidder now, so before the last two lots are sold he gives his limits on them too.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "Well played, Reina. You said sorry to my face, the way I did to you, and I'll take it the same way. Here's my arithmetic. I have sixty, I hold nothing, and I need five pieces plus twenty to spare. So I'll go to twenty on the Bowl and twenty-two on the Vase and not a grain more. Clark, Rich, if either of you wants it more than that, it's yours, and I won't push your price up just to spite you. And Reina, ten a year is very fair, and I'll hold you to it, the same way I'd have held myself.",
 "Noah: I'm starting the Scholar's set from nothing, so I'll take a piece if it comes cheap, but I won't empty my purse in a fight between Clark and Rich over who gets the throne next. I lost the throne by keeping my word, and I mean to win it back the same way.")
DN = "I let it go, keep my grain for next year's set, and bid nothing beyond my ceilings."
lot(6, "WB", [B(4,20), B(4,None,DN), B(3,21)],
 hover="Noah stopped at 20; Rich's limit was 60. Rich puts the Wooden Bowl on L14.", at="L14")
act("Rich's set is complete after the sixth lot, with the Vase still to be sold, so the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places now.", map_hl=[3])
say("Rich", "Paddy, thank you, a beautiful Bowl at an honest price. Reina, my dear, you held that chair for a whole lot and a half, and you sat in it beautifully. I'm terribly sorry, but a landlord who has his whole set in hand doesn't leave it lying on the table while Clark is bidding on a Vase. You'll find Province 3 a well-kept house, and the rent is five, just as I always promised.",
 "Rich: Clark was one Vase from doing to me exactly what I've just done to Reina, so I'm not giving him the chance. Now I hold the throne, the reserve, and every promise Noah made, and the first thing I'll have to do is find out which of them I can afford to keep.")
swap(3, 1, "Richard Baron shows the Gentry's complete set (Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Vase, Wooden Bowl) and 76.5 grain, after the sixth lot and before the Vase is sold, and takes the throne. He returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 149.5. Reina Osorio draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Gentry, in Province 3, from now on.",
     hover="Clark has won the Vase on the limits already given, but it isn't sold yet, so his set isn't complete when Rich moves. Rich is no longer a bidder, so his keep-from on the Vase lapses.")
act("Reina is a bidder now, so before the Vase is sold she gives her limit on it too. Rich, on the throne, no longer bids, so his keep-from lapses.", map_hl=[3])
say("Reina", "Lot and a half. I'll have that carved somewhere, Rich. And I'll remember you said 'terribly sorry,' the way I remember everything else. Fine. Province 3, the Harvest Pile, the rent book. I've run a whole empire, so I can run a granary. Clark, the Vase is yours if you want it. I won't spend a grain to stop you, and I suspect His new Majesty will be the one most interested in how that bid ends.",
 "Reina: Sixty grain and five pieces to buy, so a bidding war tonight would only feed the Pile. If Clark finishes his set, the throne he takes is Rich's, not mine, and I'm happy to watch that. Then I start again, and this time I'll control the payout and the rent while everyone else spends.")
lot(7, "VA", [B(4,22), B(4,None,DN), B(2,23)],
 hover="Noah stopped at 22; Clark's limit was 60. Clark puts the Vase on J16.", at="J16")
act("Clark's set is complete after the last lot, so the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places now.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "J16, at the end of the row, where it makes five. Rich, my dear old friend, you held the chair for one lot, which is still more than Reina got, so you can take some comfort in that. It's terribly sorry, I'm sure, but the man who collected the crown's tax now keeps the crown. You'll find Province 2 well kept, and I'll pay my Official at least five. Out of everyone at this table, you know how much that job's worth.",
 "Clark: Three Emperors in one auction, and the last man to sit down is the one who kept his mouth shut, kept his tally, and saved enough grain for it. I'm closing the other book tonight, with a fat reserve, a Wall to build, and Rich doing the collecting.")
swap(2, 1, "Clark Grafton shows the Official's complete set (Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 69 grain, after the last lot, and takes the throne. He returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 226. Richard Baron draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Official, in Province 2, from now on.",
     hover="Three changes of throne in one auction: Reina after lot 5, Rich after lot 6, Clark after lot 7.")
act("Reina and Rich each held the throne for one lot: Reina from after lot 5 to after lot 6, Rich from after lot 6 to after lot 7.", map_hl=[1])
say("Clark", "Noah, ten. You paid me ten every year you sat here and never missed a grain, so you're in the good book and you're staying there. Rich, five, the minimum. That's the going rate for a man who was terribly sorry over forty-one grain of Vase. Collect my tax cleanly for a year and I'll look at it again.",
 "Clark: Noah has no grain and no pieces, so a little kindness costs me nothing and buys me a friend. Rich gets the minimum and a reason to behave, because now he's the one who has to earn his way into the good book.")
salaries({2:5, 4:10}, text="Salaries: Clark, now the Emperor, pays the new Official, Rich, 5 and the new Scholar, Noah, 10.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: One Rung at a Time
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "A bad harvest", "dribble2")
act("Noah, the Scholar, turns the card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and nobody plays a personal disaster. The population stays at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[4], big=True)
say("Noah", "Year 5's card: Bad Harvest. \"The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame.\" Every province collects a bad harvest this year, and no province plays a personal disaster. Population stays at x2, so the counter reads 200 million. Read the x2 row, bad-harvest column. Clark, that's a lean year for the crown's tax too, so please budget for the 850 of works still owed.",
 "Noah: My card shows an average year already costs me 5, so a bad harvest only makes that worse, and every peasant at this table is about to feel it as well. I'll be watching who has the grain left to buy land before the next population card puts us all on the x4 row.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it.")
say("Reina", "Bad harvest, so you get the lean rate: five each, Silke, Paddy, Graham, and not a grain more this year. I keep a long book, and I remember who stood by me when the rains came late. And Clark, my province sits under four Wall links with not one stone laid. I'll keep asking.",
 "Reina: I'm at thirty grain and losing five a year with no land, so I need every coin, but squeezing the peasants in a famine year only feeds a revolt that nobody here can afford. Fifteen keeps me afloat, the peasants owe me a little goodwill, and the next time I ask for more I'll be asking people I've treated well.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina, the Gentry, asks the minimum, and each peasant pays her 5.")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, and only the coast and the wheat pay it, since nobody owes tax on a loss. That's nine grain each, friends, which buys you a rainy year at the crown's expense. Reina, you've asked four times, so I wrote it down four times. Two links of your Wall: the crown lays sixty and you lay twenty, and the stones go up with your name on them too. Anyone breaking new ground this year pays five a square, and the crown covers the other fifteen. Noah, ten again, good book. Rich, five, other book, and he knows the page.",
 "Clark: Every square I pay for is a province that owes me tax next year, and every stone on Reina's Wall is one she can't say I never laid. I'm not buying her goodwill, I'm buying her a debt.")
act("Only Silke, Paddy and Graham can buy new land this year, besides the crown: Rich's and Reina's strips open only once all four Wall links above them stand, and no free square touches Province 4.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
taxes(.30, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Rich", "Nine from the coast, nine from the rice, nine from the wheat. Twenty-seven, counted twice and not a grain shaved off, Your Majesty. Silke, Paddy, Graham: I know what a lean year costs, and I'm not taking a half-grain more than the decree says. Write that in the good book too, Clark, the page with my name on it.",
 "Rich: Five a year, and I'm sitting on thirty-five grain with nothing in my hand, so the only thing I've got to sell is being honest, and I'm making sure the man with two hundred and seventy grain sees it. The peasants have the money this year, and the day the Wall goes up over my province I'll want them remembering who was gentle with them in the bad harvest.")
act("The Wall above Province 3 is a negotiated work, so the Storyteller asks Reina whether she pays the 20 Clark asks of her.", map_hl=[3])
say("Reina", "Four times asked and once answered. Thank you, Clark, truly, and I'll take the stones. But two links don't open a single square for me, and I'm a landlord with no land and forty-five grain. Ten from me, seventy from a crown sitting on nearly three hundred, and my name goes on them gladly.",
 "Reina: Half a Wall is a promise, not a harvest, so I'll put in enough to show good faith and keep the rest for the set and the lean years. If Clark balks over ten grain, the whole table sees what his \"good book\" is worth.")
say("Clark", "Twenty-seven, counted twice. Very well, Rich, I'll write it down. In pencil, mind, on the good page, and it stays there if next year's count is as clean. The crown takes F23 and F24, forty grain out of the reserve, and from next harvest they pay for themselves. Silke, Paddy, Graham, the offer stands: you lay five a square and the crown lays fifteen.",
 "Clark: Two squares for me is forty grain I'll have back in two harvests, and every peasant square I pay for is a province that owes the crown a bigger tax and a favour. Rich has started asking for the good book, and a man who asks for it can be kept asking.")
buy_land(1, ["F23", "F24"], {1:40}, "New land: Clark buys two squares of sea for Province 1, at F23 and F24, for 40 from the crown. Each adds 20 to the Emperor's harvest from next year.")
say("Clark", "Ten grain, Reina. Done, the stones go up, and it goes in the book exactly like that: the crown seventy, the Gentry ten, two links of Wall above Province 3. When the last two links are laid and your strip opens, I'll want to know who you remember standing by you in a late rain. The crown also takes E23 and F25, so the north-east doesn't go hungry if the counter climbs.",
 "Clark: She thinks she beat me down by ten grain, and what she really did was put her name under seventy of mine. Two more squares for me as well, because if that population card turns over, the crown wants to be eating before anyone else is.")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:70, 3:10}, wall={3:2}, text="Public works: Clark builds the first two links of the Great Wall above Province 3, at 80: the crown pays 70 and Reina, the Gentry, 10.", hover="Paid into the Harvest Pile. Two of the four links above Province 3 now stand; its strip opens for new land once all four do. 770 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["E23", "F25"], {1:40}, "New land: Clark buys two more squares of sea for Province 1, at E23 and F25, for 40 from the crown. Province 1 now has six squares of new land.")
act("New land, for each province that can buy: Silke, Paddy and Graham are asked, with the crown paying 15 of every 20.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Five a square and the crown lays fifteen. Clark, I heard you, and I'll take it four times over: K26, L27, M27, N27. That's twenty from me and sixty from a treasury that's spent eighty on stones today without blinking. Write it in whichever book you like, Your Majesty. The coast feeds the crown either way.",
 "Silke: Twenty grain buys me eighty more a year from next harvest, and that covers the two squares I'd need if the counter jumps to x4, with two to spare. I still keep fifty-one for the auction. Everyone else at this table is spending to hold a chair, and I'm spending to make sure I'm the one with grain left when those chairs come up for sale.")
act("The crown paid 70 of the Wall's 80, not 80; Reina paid the other 10.", map_hl=[1,3])
buy_land(5, ["K26", "L27", "M27", "N27"], {5:20, 1:60}, "New land: Silke buys four squares of sea for Province 5, at K26, L27, M27 and N27, paying 5 a square (20); the crown pays 15 a square (60). Each adds 20 to Province 5's harvest from next year.")
say("Paddy", "Two more by the shore, Clark, S27 and T27, five apiece from me and fifteen from the crown, just as you offered, and thank you for it. It's a bad year to be planting, I know, but the rice doesn't care what the card said when it comes up next spring.",
 "Paddy: Ten grain gets me forty grain of field, and if the counter jumps to x4 I'll still be eating. I'm keeping the other forty-odd back, though, because there's a Deed, a Jar and a Vase still to be won, and I'm not going to be outbid by one grain again.")
buy_land(6, ["S27", "T27"], {6:10, 1:30}, "New land: Paddy buys two squares of sea for Province 6, at S27 and T27, paying 10; the crown pays 30. Each adds 20 to Province 6's harvest from next year.")
say("Graham", "Five a square, Your Majesty, as you said it. Three squares on the southern edge: fifteen from me, forty-five from the crown. I'll write it down in my book, the same as you do in yours.",
 "Graham: Five grain buys a square that pays twenty every year after, and you don't turn down a bargain like that from a man who's counting his friends. I'm stopping at three, though, because the Customs House, the Jar and the Bowl will cost what this table charges, and I've learned not to show up at an auction short.")
buy_land(7, ["T14", "T17", "U15"], {7:15, 1:45}, "New land: Graham buys three squares for Province 7, at T14, T17 and U15, paying 15; the crown pays 45. Each adds 20 to Province 7's harvest from next year.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives its limits for all six lots at once and sets the minimum on its own.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Rich", "My Customs House goes up at two, friends, the honest price, and I'm not making anyone pay for the privilege. As for the rest, I'm a humble tax-man with thirty-five grain and an empty shelf, so I'll be bidding like a church mouse. Don't any of you go to war over a Bowl in a bad-harvest year; the Pile's fat enough already.",
 "Rich: I've got nothing, so this year I'm buying cheap or not at all, and I'm only reaching a bit higher for the Brush because there are just two left and Reina and Silke both want one. Five small pieces at bargain prices is worth more to me than one I paid through the nose for, and I'm keeping grain back this time, as I promised myself.")
say("Reina", "I've spent two years losing bidding wars, and every grain we throw at each other goes into the pile I'm sitting on. So I'm bidding small and honest, and my Deed goes up at two for whoever wants it. Anyone who wants a piece I'm after, come and talk to me before the gavel, not after.",
 "Reina: Thirty-five grain won't buy a set this year, so I'm buying the scarce pieces cheap: there are only two Brushes left, so that's where I'll stretch. The rest I'll pick up only if the table is too poor to bother.")
say("Noah", "Before we bid, here's the sum. The crown has 12 and owes 15 in salaries this year, ten to me and five to Rich, so it's three short unless something comes in. Clark, I'm not asking you to pay me first. I just want it said out loud before it turns into a surprise. As for me, I've got 40 grain and no land, and I'm bidding small on everything. If any of you would rather sell me a piece cheap than drive the price up, the Harvest Pile gets three quarters of whatever we fight over.",
 "Noah: If I win all five at my top prices I've spent 27 and still have 13, which isn't a set and isn't the 20 I need, but it's a start that won't put me in debt. I'm watching the crown more than the lots, because at 12 grain Clark is one bad card from not being able to pay anyone.")
say("Silke", "Crown's at twelve grain and the Gentry and the Official are at thirty-five, so this is a cheap year for anyone who planted while the planting was good. The Jars are nine, friends. Every set at this table needs one, and mine doesn't.",
 "Silke: Fifty-one grain spread over five lots, every bid odd so I can't lose on a tie to Rich again. Graham is the only one at this table richer than me, and he still needs that Customs House, so that's the one I'll pay a little extra to keep away from him.")
say("Paddy", "I'll say it plainly so nobody's surprised: I'm after the Deed, the Jar and the Vase, and I've a fair bit more in my purse than I had last year. Reina, you beat me to the Deed by one grain last time. I'm not complaining, mind, I'm just telling you I remember. The Bowl starts at ten this year. Clark, I know your purse is thin, so there's no hurry on it.",
 "Paddy: Forty-two and a half grain spread over three pieces. The Deed gets the most because that's where I lost out last time, and the Jar and the Vase get thirteen each. If I take even two of the three this year, I'm one good harvest off the set, and that's a strange thing for a rice farmer to be saying out loud.")
say("Graham", "I'll bid on the Customs House, the Jar and the Bowl, and nothing else. Whoever wants them can see what they cost.",
 "Graham: The whole table's short this year, and I've got the most grain I've ever had in my hand, so I'm putting it on the Customs House first and taking whatever's left of the Jar and Bowl. Next year the south brings in a hundred and fifteen, and I can wait one more round for whatever I don't get.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed 2, Brush 2, Dragon Jar 9, Wooden Bowl 10, Vase 10.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DR = "Too rich for a tax-man's purse. Take it with my blessing."
DE = "Take it, it's yours. I'll remember the price."
DN = "Too rich for a man with no land. Take it, and good luck to you."
DS = "Take it, then. I'll be the one holding grain next harvest."
DP = "Fair enough, it's yours. I'll be back for the next one."
DG = "Not at that price. I'll be back next year."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,5), B(3,None,DE), B(4,6), B(4,None,DN), B(5,15,None,"Silke's last 2 were a keep-from, to stop Graham."), B(5,None,DS), B(7,16)],
 hover="Silke went to 15 to keep it from Graham; Graham's limit was 28. Graham puts the Customs House on O15.", at="O15")
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,6), B(2,None,DR), B(4,6), B(4,None,DN), B(5,9), B(5,None,DS), B(6,10)],
 hover="Silke stopped at 9; Paddy's limit was 16. Paddy puts the Land Deed Scroll on S23.", at="S23")
lot(4, "BB", [B(3,7), B(3,None,DE), B(2,8), B(2,None,DR), B(5,9)],
 hover="Rich stopped at 8; Silke's limit was 11. Silke puts the Bamboo Brush on O22.", at="O22")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,13), B(6,None,DP), B(7,14)],
 hover="Paddy stopped at 13; Graham's limit was 20. Graham puts the Dragon Jar on P14.", at="P14")
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,10)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 10, and nobody else bid that high (Silke 9). Graham puts the Wooden Bowl on P17.", at="P17")
lot(7, "VA", [B(6,10)],
 hover="Graham's minimum was 10, and nobody else bid that high (Silke 7). Paddy puts the Vase on R24.", at="R24")
say("Paddy", "Ten and ten. Reina, Graham, thank you both, and I'll not pretend I'm not surprised. I came in braced to lose the Deed by a grain again. That's four of the five sitting in the rice now, and all I'm short is a Jar. Graham, you've a good eye for them, I'll give you that.",
 "Paddy: Four pieces, twenty-odd grain left and a Jar away from the set, and I'm the rice farmer. Graham's got two Jars' worth of everybody's attention now, so next year I'll have to talk to Silke before the gavel and not after it.")
say("Silke", "Nine, Rich. One grain over your eight. I told myself two years ago a tie with you is worth nothing to me, so I stopped bidding even numbers. And Graham, a Customs House, a Deed, a Brush, a Jar and a Bowl. One Vase short of a chair. I can count, and so can everyone else here.",
 "Silke: First piece on my shelf after five years, and I've set it inland where the sea can't reach it. The real story this year is Graham, who's one Vase from a full set with fifty-nine grain spent down, so I've just pointed the whole table at him before he gets to point at one of us.")
act("Graham's set needs no Vase: the Wheat Peasant collects every type but his own. With the Customs House, Deed, Brush, Jar and Bowl he holds all five, and his own Vase's quarter (2.5) takes him from 19 to 21.5, past the 20 the set needs. So after the last lot the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places.", map_hl=[7])
say("Graham", "The Customs House, the Deed, the Brush, the Jar and the Bowl, all five in the middle of the wheat. Noah, you said sorry to us peasants two years ago. I wrote it down. Now the Scholar's chair is mine, and the wheat is yours.",
 "Graham: Five years of bidding sensibly, then one bad-harvest year when everyone else was broke, and I took three pieces for forty grain. I'm taking the Scholar's seat because Noah has the most grain of the three upper-class seats, the crown has to pay that chair, and the man who reads the I Ching gets to watch the Emperor's book from close up.")
swap(7, 4, "Graham Garner shows the Wheat Peasant's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl) and 21.5 grain, after the last lot, and replaces Noah as the Scholar: the first peasant to climb. He takes Noah's 42 (63.5 in all), returns the Brush to Province 4's stock and carries the Customs House, Deed, Jar and Bowl to Province 4 (K21, L21, K22, L22). Noah Tall draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Wheat Peasant, in Province 7, from now on.",
     hover="As Scholar, Graham collects every type but the Brush, so he now needs only a Vase. The Scholar's jobs pass with the seat: from Year 6 Graham reads the I Ching, and the Emperor owes him the Scholar's salary.",
     at={"CH":"K21", "LD":"L21", "DJ":"K22", "WB":"L22"})
say("Graham", "Silke, I counted it too. I needed no Vase, I needed the twenty, and the Vase paid it. Paddy, you're one Jar short now, and I know what that feels like, so I'll keep quiet about it. Noah, the wheat's good land. The south has a Reservoir and five new squares, and nobody charges rent on the Reservoir.",
 "Graham: Four pieces carried north into the Scholar's province, set close together in the middle, with only a Vase still to find. Next I'm watching Paddy, one Jar from a chair, and Clark, who now has to pay my salary with twelve grain in the treasury.")
act("Salaries: the crown has 12 and Clark declared Official 5 and Scholar 10, which is 15. The Scholar's salary now goes to Graham. The Storyteller asks Clark how he pays.", map_hl=[1,2,4])
say("Clark", "Five to Rich and five to Graham, the lawful minimum, and the crown keeps two grain and its dignity. Graham, I promised ten to the Scholar, but the man I promised it to is out planting wheat now, so you get the chair's rate until you've earned the book's rate. Noah, you're still in the good book. I just can't afford to prove it this year. And I'll say the next part plainly: every grain the crown spent this year went into your squares and Reina's Wall, so when the harvest comes in, I'll expect to hear about it.",
 "Clark: I paid for nine peasant squares and two of Reina's links, and one of those peasants took a chair while I watched, so that's a new page in the other book. With two grain left I have to call in every favour I bought this year before somebody counts the reserve and decides it's their turn.")
salaries({2:5, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Clark pays the Official, Rich, 5 and the new Scholar, Graham, 5, the minimum each. The crown ends the year at 2.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Fortune and misfortune", "goodevil")
act("Graham, the new Scholar, turns the card: Fortune and Misfortune. Every province collects a good harvest, and Provinces 2, 4 and 6 each play their next personal disaster (Province 8 is empty). The population stays at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2,4,6], big=True)
say("Graham", "Year 6's card: Fortune and Misfortune. \"Plenty for everyone, and trouble for some.\" Every province collects a good harvest. Provinces 2, 4, 6 and 8 play a personal disaster: that's Rich, me and Paddy, and there's no Province 8. Population stays at x2, so the counter reads 200 million. Read the x2 row, good-harvest column. Clark, five is the chair's rate and I'll take it. I'll read the card the same way at five as I would at ten.",
 "Graham: The first card I've read, and it names my own province for trouble, which is an honest start. Clark has two grain and a long memory, and I need only a Vase now, so I'll keep the book straight and watch who bids on Noah's.")
disaster(2, "the Official's best horse goes lame on the road to collect the tax, and a new one has to be bought.")
disaster(4, "the Scholar's study roof leaks onto his calendar of the stars, and the scrolls must be copied out again.")
disaster(6, "the Rice Peasant's only water buffalo breaks its leg, and the planting stops.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the good harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Last year I charged five because the seed rotted. This year the granaries are full, so the rent follows the harvest: fifteen from the coast, fifteen from the wheat, and ten from the rice, because Paddy said thank you out loud and I keep that book too. Noah, welcome to the wheat. I hope the land is as kind to you as you once were to me.",
 "Reina: The peasants are sitting on more than five hundred grain between them while I'm still waiting on a Wall, so I've raised the rent, but gently enough that nobody has a reason to fight me over it. Paddy is one Jar short of a set and is the man most likely to climb next, so his discount buys me a friend for when he does.")
act("Rent above the minimum of 5 is a negotiation, so each peasant may pay what Reina asks (Silke 15, Paddy 10, Noah 15) or only the 5 the rules require. Each answers when the land is offered, later this year.", map_hl=[3,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "A good harvest, so a proper tax: forty percent, from everyone with a harvest. Rich, count it the way you counted last year and you'll get ten at the end of the year, out of the good book. Reina, the crown finishes your Wall: the last two links, sixty from me and twenty from you, and then your strip is open and you're a landlord with land. I'll remember who laid the last stone. Land is ten and ten this year, friends. I bought you nine squares last year and I'd like to see a harvest out of them first.",
 "Clark: Last year I emptied the treasury into everyone's fields and ended with two grain, so this year the crown gets paid back first and the generosity comes in halves. Finishing Reina's Wall leaves her in my debt for every square she plants under it, and a raise for Rich keeps the tax-man counting for me rather than for himself.")
taxes(.40, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Rich", "Forty percent, and I'll start with my own purse so nobody says the tax-man spared himself: eight from Rich Baron, eight from Reina, eight from Graham, sixty-four from the coast, forty-eight from the rice, fifty-six from the wheat. A hundred and ninety-two, Your Majesty, counted twice, and every one of them is right to the half-grain. And Silke, nine over my eight was well bid. I've written down that you don't bid even numbers.",
 "Rich: Ten a year is the only raise I'm likely to get this decade, so I'm counting like a saint and paying my own share first where Clark can see it. Meanwhile Paddy is one Jar short of a chair and Graham showed us all that a peasant can climb, so next year I'm watching the Jars, not the throne.")
act("The last two links of the Wall above Province 3 are a negotiated work, so the Storyteller asks Reina whether she pays the 20 Clark asks, and, if the Wall goes up, whether she buys land under it.", map_hl=[3])
say("Reina", "Twenty, Clark, gladly, and you can put that in ink, not pencil. Six years I've waited for that Wall, and the first thing I'll do with it is plant: G9 and G10, ten apiece, under stones that carry both our names.",
 "Reina: Finally a landlord with land. Two squares put me past break-even, and I keep a little grain back until the rent comes in. Clark has earned a real mark in my book this year, and I'll pay it back the next time the throne is in play.")
build("Great Wall", 80, {1:60, 3:20}, wall={3:2}, text="Public works: Clark builds the last two links of the Great Wall above Province 3, at 80: the crown pays 60 and Reina, the Gentry, 20. All four links above Province 3 now stand, and its strip under the Wall opens for new land.", hover="Paid into the Harvest Pile. 690 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["E24", "E25"], {1:40}, "New land: Clark buys two more squares of sea for Province 1, at E24 and E25, for 40 from the crown.")
buy_land(3, ["G9", "G10"], {3:20, 1:20}, "New land: Reina buys the first two squares of Province 3's strip under the Wall, G9 and G10, paying 10 a square; the crown pays the other 10 of each.")
act("New land, for each peasant: Silke, Paddy and Noah are asked, with the crown paying 10 of every 20, and each answers Reina's rent at the same time.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Five, Reina, the lawful rent, the same five you charged me when the seed rotted. You keep a long book and so do I, and mine says the coast paid sixty-four in tax this year and planted the crown's land on its own back. Clark, ten and ten is fair: three more squares on the shore, and you'll see their harvest next year in Rich's count.",
 "Silke: Reina's down to nine and a half grain on a seat I can take, and I'm not going to pay to fatten her up. And Paddy's one Jar short of a full set, while I'm the only one at this table who sells Jars, so this year the Jar price is going to tell the room who I'm keeping out.")
buy_land(5, ["O27", "P27", "Q26"], {5:30, 1:30}, "New land: Silke buys three squares of sea for Province 5, at O27, P27 and Q26, paying 30; the crown pays 30.")
say("Paddy", "Ten it is, Reina. You asked fair and you asked kindly, and I'd sooner pay a fair rent than haggle over five grain in a full year. Clark, one square, T24, ten and ten as you said. I'll show you a harvest out of the others before I ask for more. And Silke, I'll say it to your face, not behind your back: I'm after a Jar this year.",
 "Paddy: Twenty out of ninety-three, so seventy-three left for one Dragon Jar and the twenty the set needs, which is more than I've ever had going into the auctions. If Silke's minded to talk before the gavel, I'm listening.")
buy_land(6, ["T24"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys one square for Province 6, at T24, paying 10; the crown pays 10.")
say("Noah", "Reina, here's fifteen, the full amount you asked. I'm not paying it out of gratitude. Two years ago I said I'd make it right with you and I haven't yet, and I won't call this rent the payment. That debt is still open. Clark, three squares at ten each from me and ten each from the crown: T13, U14, U16. That's thirty from me and thirty from you, and I have 99 left after it. Graham, the wheat is good land. I only wish the man who used to read the cards had looked at the map this carefully when he was buying.",
 "Noah: I've got 144 grain and land that already pays, and I won't save my way to a chair by sitting on it. So I pay Reina in full, plant three squares at half price, and keep about a hundred for next year's auctions. A Customs House, a Deed, a Brush, a Jar and a Bowl, at fair prices.")
rents(5, "Rents: each peasant pays Reina, the Gentry, the minimum 5.")
act("Paddy pays Reina the extra 5 she asked of him and Noah the extra 10, so Reina collects 5 from Silke, 10 from Paddy and 15 from Noah: 30 in all.", deltas={6:-5, 4:-10, 1:15}, map_hl=[3,6,7], deal=True)
buy_land(7, ["T13", "U14", "U16"], {7:30, 1:30}, "New land: Noah buys three squares for Province 7, at T13, U14 and U16, paying 30; the crown pays 30.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives its limits for all six lots at once and sets the minimum on its own. Graham, now the Scholar, sells the Brushes; Noah, now the Wheat Peasant, the Vases.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Rich", "Customs House at two again, friends; I don't overcharge the people I tax. Paddy, you told Silke to her face you're after a Jar, so I'll be just as honest with you: I need one too, and I'm not standing aside for it. Nothing personal. You're a fine tenant and a finer bidder, and I'd sooner lose it to you dear than give it to you cheap.",
 "Rich: Paddy is one Jar from a chair, and last year I watched a peasant climb while everyone else was looking somewhere else, so I'll push that Jar to twenty-five to make him pay for it. I went to eleven on the Brush because Silke beat me by one grain last time, and I don't get caught on the same number twice.")
say("Reina", "Let's count out loud, since Silke likes counting: Paddy is one Jar from a chair and Graham is one Vase from another, and both have the grain to sit down in them. I'm not saying who to stop. I'm only saying that whoever lets it through shouldn't be surprised by whose chair gets taken.",
 "Reina: I can't buy a set this year, but I can make the two men closest to one pay for it, and everyone who's afraid of losing a seat should be bidding alongside me. My grain is for blocking the Jar and the Vase, and anything I pick up cheap for my own shelf is a bonus.")
say("Graham", "One piece, friends, and I'll say which: the Vase, and nothing else. Paddy, I won't bid against you on the Jar, and I'd ask the same courtesy back, as one man who came up from the fields to another: a set that's finished doesn't have to come for the chair I'm sitting in. My Brush goes up at eight.",
 "Graham: If I get the Vase and keep twenty, I'm holding a full set and I'm not a peasant any more, and you know what the Scholar's set can reach. The only man who can stop me first is Paddy, one Jar from a set, so I'm offering him the Jar in public and hoping he looks up at the throne instead of at my chair.")
say("Silke", "Paddy, you said it to my face and I respect that, so here it is to yours: the Jar is twenty-one this year. You're one piece from a chair, and that's what a chair costs. And Noah, you've got ninety-nine grain and an empty shelf, so I expect we'll be bidding against each other on the Customs House.",
 "Silke: Win all four lots tonight and I keep twenty-six and a half, enough to climb straight out of the coast, with every limit odd so Rich's book is no use to anyone. The Jar at twenty-one means Paddy pays dearly for his set or doesn't get it, and either way a quarter of it ends up with me.")
say("Paddy", "I'll put my cards face up, since everybody can count anyway. I'm bidding on the Jar and nothing else this year, and I'll stay in a good long while on it. Anybody who's only after it to keep it off me will be feeding the Harvest Pile, not themselves. The Bowl's at ten again, same as last year, and it's a fair price.",
 "Paddy: Fifty-three is everything I've got except the twenty the set needs, so if someone beats that they'll have earned it and I'll shake their hand. The Bowl's quarter comes in after the Jar, so that's a couple of grain of breathing room on top.")
say("Noah", "Here's my arithmetic, so nobody has to guess it. I have 99 grain, I need five pieces, and I need 20 left over at the end. That leaves about 79 to spread across five lots, so you'll see me on all of them and you'll see me stop. Paddy, you told us you want the Jar, and I'll be honest back: so do I. I won't pretend it's worth more to me than it is. My Vase goes up at eight.",
 "Noah: Paddy is one Jar from a chair, so that's where my top bid goes. If he outbids me at 18 he's earned it, and I'll still have most of my grain for the other four. Graham did it in one night with a full purse, and I'm trying to do the same with a careful one.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 2, Land Deed 2, Brush 8, Dragon Jar 21, Wooden Bowl 10, Vase 8.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
DR = "There it goes. Spend it wisely, and remember who made you pay full price."
DE = "There it goes. Remember I'm the one who said it first."
DG = "Too rich for me this year. The Vase will come round again."
DS = "Have it. The coast will still be here next year, and so will I."
DP = "Well bid. I'll get one yet, you'll see."
DN = "That's past what my sum allows. Well bid."
act("The Brush is close at the top: Noah's limit of 12 against Rich's 11. So the two of them get one live raise each. Noah raises to 16, Rich to 15.", map_hl=[2,7])
say("Noah", "Silke beat me to the Customs House and the Deed by one grain each, and she bid fair for both. So the Brush is the one piece I can take home this year, and I'll go to sixteen for it. Rich, if you go past that, I'll shake your hand and the Pile can have the rest.",
 "Noah: Every other lot went past my ceiling. If I lose this one too, I finish the year with 99 grain and nothing on my shelf. Sixteen is still small next to a hundred, and it's the first piece toward a set.")
say("Rich", "Noah, you said you'd stop, and I believe you, you're a man of plain numbers. Mine's plainer: there are two Brushes left in this whole empire, and an Official with no Brush stays an Official forever. With apologies, I'll be staying in a little longer.",
 "Rich: The Jar's gone to Paddy and the Vase to Graham, so two peasants are about to climb and I'm leaving this year with nothing unless I get the Brush. It's the scarcest piece on the board, and at fifteen I've still got thirty-seven grain left to stop the man behind me next time.")
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,4), B(3,None,DE), B(7,15), B(7,None,DN), B(5,16)],
 hover="Noah stopped at 15; Silke's limit was 23. Silke puts the Customs House on O21.", at="O21")
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,6), B(2,None,DR), B(7,15), B(7,None,DN), B(5,16)],
 hover="Noah stopped at 15; Silke's limit was 21. Silke puts the Land Deed Scroll on O23.", at="O23")
lot(4, "BB", [B(2,15), B(2,None,DR), B(7,16)],
 hover="In the live raise Rich went to 15 and Noah to 16. Noah puts the Bamboo Brush on Q15.", at="Q15")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,25,None,"Rich's last 15 were a keep-from, to stop Paddy."), B(2,None,DR), B(6,26)],
 hover="Rich went to 25 to keep it from Paddy; Paddy's limit was 53. Paddy puts the Dragon Jar on R23.", at="R23")
say("Silke", "Noah, one grain each time, and you bid them fair. I'll take the handshake you offered Rich. Paddy, twenty-six for the Jar, and a quarter of it goes into the coast, so thank you twice. And Rich, look at that: the Brush went at sixteen, an even number, and the man in front of you still won it.",
 "Silke: That's a Customs House, a Deed and a Brush in a row down the middle of the coast, with a Bowl coming at sixteen, so I'm one Vase short. Graham beat me to this year's Vase by a single grain. There are two left in stock, and I'll have the biggest income at this table when they come up next year.")
say("Noah", "The Brush goes on Q15, in the middle of the wheat. Sixteen to the auction: four to Graham and twelve to the Pile, and that leaves me 83. Rich, you said you'd stay in and then you stopped at fifteen, and I'm not holding it against you. It's one piece, and I've got four still to find.",
 "Noah: I've put it in the centre of the province, well away from the edges, where any new land I buy would go. One piece in six years, so the arithmetic is slow, but at least it's going the right way now.")
act("Paddy's set is complete after the fifth lot, with the Bowl and the Vase still to be sold, so the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places now.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "Well, I'll be. Rich, you told me to my face you weren't standing aside, and you didn't, and you lost it dear, which is just what you said you'd sooner do. So I'll be as plain with you as you were with me: I'm taking the Official's chair. There's nothing personal in it either. Graham, you kept your word on the Jar, so I've kept clear of yours. And I'll count the tax the way Rich did, twice and to the half-grain, because that's the only way I know how to count.",
 "Paddy: A rice farmer collecting the crown's tax, my mother wouldn't believe it. I picked Rich because he was the one who stood openly against me. I left Reina alone because she charged me fair, and Graham because he kept his word, and I'd like to be the sort of Official people remember like that.")
swap(6, 2, "Paddy Doyle shows the Rice Peasant's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Vase) and 47 grain, after the fifth lot and before the Bowl and the Vase are sold, and replaces Rich as the Official. He takes Rich's 56 (103 in all), returns the Customs House to Province 2's stock and carries the Brush, Deed, Jar and Vase to Province 2 (H13, H14, I13, I14). Richard Baron draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Rice Peasant, in Province 6, from now on.",
     hover="As Official, Paddy collects every type but the Customs House, so he now needs only a Wooden Bowl, and the Bowl is the next lot. Rich now sells it, at the minimum of 10 Paddy set.",
     at={"BB":"H13", "LD":"H14", "DJ":"I13", "VA":"I14"})
act("Paddy and Rich are both bidders of a new kind now, so before the last two lots are sold each gives his limits: Paddy on the Bowl and the Vase, Rich on the Vase.", map_hl=[2,6])
say("Paddy", "Rich, there's your Bowl to sell now, and you'll get a fair quarter from me if I can manage it. I'm going after it, and I'll not pretend otherwise. The Vase is Graham's to fight over. I've one already and I've no use for two.",
 "Paddy: I sat down as Official a minute ago, and now I'm one Bowl short of a full set again. I sold those Bowls myself for three years, so I know exactly what one's worth to me. Thirty-five leaves me plenty over the twenty, so if Silke wants it more than that she can have it.")
say("Rich", "Well, Paddy, you were plain with me and I was plain with you, and here we are. Congratulations, sincerely; I told this table the Rice Peasant was the man who mattered, and I was right, only not the way I meant. The Bowl's yours to buy now, all of you, at ten, and I'll take whatever a humble rice farmer is offered for it. Graham, I need a Vase too now, so I'll make you earn yours.",
 "Rich: Sixty grain, six squares of good coast, and a landlord I'm on polite terms with, which is more than the Official's chair ever paid me. Graham will almost certainly win the Vase, but every grain I make him spend now is a grain he hasn't got when I come up behind him.")
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,15), B(7,None,DN), B(5,21), B(5,None,DS), B(2,22)],
 hover="Noah stopped at 15 and Silke at 21; Paddy's limit was 35. Rich, now the Rice Peasant, sells it and keeps a quarter. Paddy puts the Wooden Bowl on H15.", at="H15")
act("Paddy's Official's set is complete after the sixth lot, with the Vase still to be sold, so the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places now.", map_hl=[2])
say("Paddy", "Clark, I said once I'd be no enemy of yours over a Bowl, and I meant it then. It's a Bowl that's done it all the same, and I'm sorry, truly. I'll take the chair. Graham, you've the Vase coming to you and I'll not stand in the way of it. If you come for this chair I'll understand, because I came for it myself. Rich, there's twenty-two for your Bowl, the fairest money I ever spent.",
 "Paddy: Two years ago I came home with an empty cart, and now I'm sitting on the throne of China with two hundred grain in the reserve. I know full well Graham's one Vase off coming for me, so I'll enjoy it while it lasts, and I'll be a fair Emperor in the meantime.")
act("Rich keeps a quarter of the 22, 5.5; the other 16.5 went to the Harvest Pile.", map_hl=[6])
swap(2, 1, "Paddy Doyle shows the Official's complete set (Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 81 grain, after the sixth lot and before the Vase is sold, and takes the throne: the Rice Peasant who started the year with an empty chair above him. He returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 124. Clark Grafton draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Official, in Province 2, from now on.",
     hover="Two seat changes in one auction: Paddy from Rice Peasant to Official after lot 5, and from Official to Emperor after lot 6. Graham has won the Vase on the limits already given, but it isn't sold yet.")
act("Clark is a bidder now, so before the Vase is sold he gives his limit on it too.", map_hl=[2])
say("Clark", "Paddy, you said you'd be no enemy of mine over a Bowl, and then a Bowl took my chair. I'm not angry, I'm making a note of it. Graham, the Vase is yours. I won't spend sixty grain to protect a throne that isn't mine any more. I'll count the tax honestly, Your Majesty, because that's the job, and I'll expect the salary to show it.",
 "Clark: One man who came up from the fields just knocked me off the throne, and if the other wants to come and knock him off it, I'm not paying fifty-three grain to stop him. The other book has a fresh page headed Paddy Doyle, and I'm starting from nothing again, sitting under a Wall nobody has built.")
lot(7, "VA", [B(3,15,None,"Reina's last 10 were a keep-from, to stop Graham."), B(3,None,DE), B(5,15), B(5,None,DS), B(6,20), B(6,None,DP), B(4,21)],
 hover="Reina (a keep-from) and Silke stopped at 15, Rich, now the Rice Peasant, at 20; Graham's limit was 52. Clark passed. Graham puts the Vase on K20.", at="K20")
act("Graham's Scholar's set is complete after the last lot, so the Storyteller asks him whether he changes places now.", map_hl=[4])
say("Graham", "Paddy, you kept clear of my Vase and I kept clear of your Jar, and you said you'd understand if I came for the chair. I'm coming for it. Three years ago I was paying fifteen for a Deed nobody wanted. Now I'll keep the book, and I'll write it in ink. Clark, you'll get the salary the count earns, and Noah, the Scholar's chair is a good place to start from. I'd know.",
 "Graham: The wheat, then the Scholar's chair, then the throne, one rung at a time, the way I wrote it down in Year 2, and I paid this table's price for every rung. Paddy's the man to watch now, because he's done this climb once already and he knows exactly how.")
swap(4, 1, "Graham Garner shows the Scholar's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 59.5 grain, after the last lot, and takes the throne. He returns all five pieces to their provinces' stock and takes the whole imperial reserve, 205. Paddy Doyle draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Scholar, in Province 4, from now on.",
     hover="Three changes of seat in one auction, two of them the throne: Paddy to Official after lot 5, Paddy to Emperor after lot 6, Graham to Emperor after lot 7. Both of this year's Emperors began the game as peasants.")
act("Paddy, not Noah, takes the Scholar's chair: the displaced Emperor plays the seat the mover left. Noah stays the Wheat Peasant.", map_hl=[4,7])
say("Graham", "Clark declared ten for the Official and five for the Scholar this year, and a declaration from the throne doesn't change because a new man is sitting in it. Clark, here's your ten, the same as you promised Rich. Paddy, five, the chair's rate, which is what I was paid in it, and it'll rise when the book says so.",
 "Graham: I'm paying exactly what the last Emperor promised, not a grain more or less, because the table needs to see that the new crown keeps old promises. Every grain I hand out goes toward someone's set, so being generous can wait until I know who's climbing.")
salaries({2:10, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Graham, the last Emperor of the year, pays what Clark declared: the new Official, Clark, 10 and the new Scholar, Paddy, 5.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: One Grain Over

# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "Heaven has favourites", "heaven")
act("Paddy, the Scholar, turns the card: Heaven Has Favourites. Provinces 2, 4 and 5 (Clark, Paddy, Silke) collect a good harvest; Provinces 1, 3, 6 and 7 (Graham, Reina, Rich, Noah) a bad one (Province 8 is empty). Nobody plays a disaster. The population stays at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[2,4,5], big=True)
say("Paddy", "\"Heaven Has Favourites. Some fields shine, some fields sulk.\" Provinces 2, 4 and 5 have a good harvest: that's Clark, me and Silke. Provinces 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8 have a bad one: Graham, Reina, Rich and Noah, and I'm sorry, I only turn the card, I don't write it. The population stays at two hundred million, so it's times two. Read the x2 row of your income tables, everybody, and if your booklet's got a disaster waiting, play the next one.",
 "Paddy: The one year I've no fields left by the shore, my province gets the good harvest, and that'll be Heaven's idea of a joke. I've read it plain, though, and that's my job, and I'll be keeping an eye on Graham at year's end for my five.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it.")
say("Reina", "Harvest's paid, every grain as the card reads it. Rent follows the fields this year. Silke, your coast shone, so I'm asking twenty, and you can tell me again why you'd rather pay five. Rich, your fields sulked and you've only just come back to that seat, so it's the lean rate. Noah, bad year for you too, and you paid me in full last time, so it's ten. That's the good column looking after its own.",
 "Reina: Low rent for the two whose fields failed, and a stiff ask of the one with three hundred grain. I need the grain for land before that counter doubles again, and I want every tenant to see that loyalty brings the rent down.")
act("Rent above the minimum of 5 is a negotiation, so Silke (asked 20) and Noah (asked 10) may each pay what Reina asks or only the 5 the rules require. Each answers when the land is offered, later this year. Rich is asked the minimum 5.", map_hl=[3,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "Forty percent. The coast shone this year, so the coast pays the most, and every grain of it goes into stone above my own province, so nobody else pays for my Wall. Buy land if you want it. The crown pays three-quarters of every square, and I'll be counting how many of you take me up on it.",
 "Graham: Silke has three pieces and three hundred grain, and forty percent takes eighty-eight of that before Noah's Vase ever goes on the block. The Wall is the biggest work left, so I'm paying for it now while I've got the money and nobody can say I made them share it.")
act("Graham declares the year: tax at 40%; the one project is all four links of the Great Wall above Province 1, at the crown's whole cost of 160; salaries of 10 to the Official and 5 to the Scholar; and the crown pays 15 of every 20 on any province's new land this year, and buys two squares for Province 1.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.40, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Two hundred and four, counted twice, and my own eight on top of the pile so nobody can say the collector skimmed. Silke, eighty-eight. I know, the coast shone, and the sun sends a bill. Graham, it's all yours, and I wrote down what you said: 'stone above my own province.' I'll be in the book with a pencil when you get round to the four links above mine, because my strip stays shut until they're built, and a collector who can't buy a field is a collector with plenty of time to count.",
 "Clark: Every grain goes in honestly, because my best leverage this year is a clean record and a Wall nobody has built over my head. Graham's going to build his own Wall first, so I'm putting it on the table now that mine comes next, and I'm keeping my mouth shut about the five pieces I'll be bidding on.")
build("Great Wall", 160, {1:160}, wall={1:4}, text="Public works: Graham builds all four links of the Great Wall above Province 1, at the crown's whole cost of 160. Province 1's strip under the Wall opens for new land.", hover="Paid into the Harvest Pile. Province 2's four links are still unbuilt, so its strip stays shut. 530 of the works are still owed.")
say("Graham", "Two squares for Province 1, paid in full, and the crown isn't asking itself for a discount. Clark, write it down in pencil, by all means. The links above your province are in the book too, and when the stone goes up there we'll talk about who pays for it, at your table and not just mine.",
 "Graham: Forty grain now gets me forty more every harvest from next year, and that's simple arithmetic. Clark wants his Wall. I'll let him want it for one more year, because a collector who's waiting on a promise from the crown doesn't go looking for a Vase.")
buy_land(1, ["D22", "D23"], {1:40}, "New land: Graham buys two squares for Province 1, at D22 and D23, for 40 from the crown.")
act("New land, for each eligible seat, with the crown paying 15 of every 20: Reina, Silke, Rich and Noah are asked (Province 4 has no free square; Province 2's strip is still shut), and Silke and Noah answer Reina's rent at the same time.", map_hl=[3,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Graham, you said three-quarters and that you'd be counting, so count four. F9, F10, F11 and G11: five a square from me, fifteen from the crown, eighty into the pile. The desert edge gets planted and the north-west feeds itself next year, which means one province fewer coming to the throne with its hand out.",
 "Reina: Four squares at five grain each is the cheapest land this table will ever see, and it's in place before the counter goes to times four. It leaves me about thirty in hand, so a bad year won't put me in debt, and Graham's own words put it on the record that he offered.")
buy_land(3, ["F9", "F10", "F11", "G11"], {3:20, 1:60}, "New land: Reina buys four more squares of Province 3's strip, at F9, F10, F11 and G11, paying 5 a square (20); the crown pays 15 a square (60).")
say("Silke", "Reina, ten. The sun shone, so you get twice the lean rate, and that's more than you gave me back when the seed rotted. Graham, you said three-quarters of every square and that you'd be counting, so here's ten for your count: fifty from me, a hundred and fifty from the crown, two hundred into the pile, and the whole coast fed from here to the next card.",
 "Silke: Every square costs me five and pays me twenty, and every square drains the throne by fifteen, so Graham's purse gets lighter while mine gets heavier. I still keep a hundred and sixty-three for the Bowl and the Vase, and Reina, on twenty-nine and a half, is the seat I'm keeping hungry.")
buy_land(5, ["J26", "J27", "K27", "K28", "L28", "M28", "N28", "O28", "P28", "Q27"], {5:50, 1:150}, "New land: Silke buys ten squares of sea for Province 5, at J26, J27, K27, K28, L28, M28, N28, O28, P28 and Q27, paying 5 a square (50); the crown pays 15 a square (150). Province 5 now has twenty squares of new land.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, you said three-quarters and that you'd be counting, so here's four for your book: five a square from me, fifteen from the crown, eighty into the pile, and the south-east coast never comes begging again. And Reina, my dear landlord, thank you for the lean rate. I said once that rent rises with the land, and I meant it from either side of the table, so when these fields come in, we'll talk again, as friends.",
 "Rich: Four squares at five apiece is eighty grain a year for twenty, and it leaves me nearly a hundred to bid with, because next time I'm keeping enough back to stop the man behind me. Graham's already paid out for Silke's ten, so I'm asking for four and not ten, and a crown that's been asked for little remembers who asked politely.")
buy_land(6, ["T22", "T23", "U23", "U24"], {6:20, 1:60}, "New land: Rich buys four squares for Province 6, at T22, T23, U23 and U24, paying 5 a square (20); the crown pays 15 a square (60).")
say("Noah", "Reina, ten it is, and thank you for the lean column. It still doesn't settle what I owe you, and I'll keep saying so until it does. Graham, here are six for your count: V13 to V17 and U17. That's thirty from me, ninety from the crown, a hundred and twenty into the pile. Add up what's been asked of you today: Reina sixty, Silke a hundred and fifty, Rich sixty, me ninety. That's three hundred and sixty out of the crown, so tell us now if the treasury can't cover it, before the stone needs paying for.",
 "Noah: Six squares at five each is a hundred and twenty more grain every harvest, and I still keep a hundred and thirty-eight for the auction, where I need four pieces. I'm saying the crown's total out loud because Graham offered three-quarters to everyone, and somebody ought to check that he can pay it.")
buy_land(7, ["V13", "V14", "V15", "V16", "V17", "U17"], {7:30, 1:90}, "New land: Noah buys six squares for Province 7, at V13, V14, V15, V16, V17 and U17, paying 5 a square (30); the crown pays 15 a square (90). The crown has paid 360 toward this year's land, and the Wall's 160 is already paid; the reserve stands at 3.5, with 15 of salaries declared for the end of the year.")
rents(5, "Rents: each peasant pays Reina, the Gentry, the minimum 5.")
act("Silke and Noah each pay Reina 5 more than the minimum, 10 each of the 20 and 10 asked, so Reina collects 10 from Silke, 5 from Rich and 10 from Noah: 25 in all.", deltas={5:-5, 4:-5, 1:10}, map_hl=[3,5,6,7], deal=True)
say("Clark", "Customs House at three, same as always. It's a toll, not a fortune. And while everyone's busy congratulating the crown on its generosity, let the record show the treasury holds three and a half grain and owes my salary and Paddy's out of it. Graham, I've been the man with two grain in that chair. I'll be at the door at year's end with my hand out, and I'll say it politely, once.",
 "Clark: I'm starting from nothing, so I'm buying cheap and quietly, the way the peasants did. The only real money is on the Bowl and the Vase, because Silke needs only those two and she's sitting on a hundred and fifty grain. If she gets one, the other book gets a new name.")
say("Reina", "My Deed starts at five, because paper with a seal on it shouldn't go for the price of a bowl of noodles. As for the rest, I'll bid like a landlord: small, patient, and with a long memory. Silke, the coast is two pieces from a chair, and everybody at this table can count to two.",
 "Reina: I'm five pieces short, so I'm not chasing a set this year, only a cheap Brush. My real grain is held back to stop Silke, who holds the Brush, Customs House and Deed and needs only a Bowl and a Vase to take somebody's seat.")
say("Paddy", "I'm starting from an empty shelf again, so I'll do it the way that worked last time: two pieces, chosen early, and I'll not chase a cart down the road. The Brush starts at five. It's a good Brush, and you've all seen what it led to the last time I sold one.",
 "Paddy: Seventy-seven grain and no fields means I can't afford a war. I'm after the Customs House and the Deed while there's stock, I'll have a cheap go at the Vase, and I'm keeping a good forty back so I don't go hungry.")
say("Silke", "I'll save you all the guessing: I'm after the Bowl and the Vase and nothing else, and every grain I've got is for those two. Anyone who wants to make me pay for them can go ahead, three-quarters of it lands in the pile either way. The Jar starts at fifteen, because every set at this table needs one and mine doesn't.",
 "Silke: The ceilings add up to a hundred and thirty-two, which leaves me twenty-one, one over the twenty I need to stand up and take a seat. Noah's the only one with the grain to fight me for the Bowl, and he's a long way from finishing a set, so the question is how much he wants to spend just to stop me.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, three and a half in the treasury and every one of us fed out of it. I'll toast the crown's generosity tonight and I'll be careful with my own. As for the auctions, friends, a humble rice farmer bids small and bids often. And Silke, I've watched your pieces go down on O21, O22, O23, so you'll forgive me if I take an interest in the Vase.",
 "Rich: Five pieces at fair, unrounded prices, because I've lost twice by exactly one grain and I'm done bidding the numbers other men guess. The real money is held back for Silke's Vase, since she's the one sitting closest to a set, and this time I keep the grain to stop the man behind me.")
say("Noah", "Here are my numbers, same as every year. I've got a hundred and thirty-eight, I need four pieces and twenty to spare, so that's a hundred and eighteen to spend, and I'll go up to twenty-eight on each of the four, no higher. Nobody gets a keep-from out of me. And Graham, the crown has three and a half and has declared fifteen in salaries, so I'll ask now what I asked last time: where is the other eleven and a half coming from?",
 "Noah: Twenty-eight a piece for four pieces comes to a hundred and twelve, which leaves me twenty-six and a full set if the table lets me have it. Silke has a hundred and fifty-three and only needs the Bowl, so if she wants it badly she'll get it, and I won't burn grain trying to stop her.")
act("The minimums: Customs House 3, Land Deed 5, Brush 5, Dragon Jar 15, Wooden Bowl 9, Vase 10.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
D7C = "Go on, then. I'm writing down what you paid."
D7E = "Take it, then. I'm writing down what it cost you."
D7P = "Fair enough, it's yours. I said I'd not chase it."
D7S = "Take it, then. The coast's still harvesting next year, and I'll have the grain to come back for it."
D7R = "Take it with my blessing, and pay the pile its three-quarters."
D7N = "That's past my number. Fair price to you, and I'll be back for the next one."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,4), B(3,None,D7E), B(6,13), B(6,None,D7R), B(4,14), B(4,None,D7P), B(7,15)],
 hover="Reina stopped at 4, Rich at 13 and Paddy at 14; Noah's limit was 28. Noah puts the Customs House on Q14.", at="Q14")
